WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:40.146
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:40.416 --> 00:41.584
A face...

3
00:44.587 --> 00:45.880
...a fleet...

4
00:48.466 --> 00:49.550
...a war...

5
00:53.262 --> 00:54.430
...a man...

6
00:57.641 --> 00:59.018
...a thought...

7
01:02.188 --> 01:03.147
...a trick...

8
01:06.233 --> 01:08.569
...a trick to break the walls of Troy.

9
01:11.113 --> 01:13.866
And burn it screaming to the ground!

10
02:05.376 --> 02:06.544
What does it mean?

11
02:15.302 --> 02:16.554
A gift.

12
02:19.723 --> 02:21.058
For Athena.

13
02:24.103 --> 02:25.354
Common gods.

14
02:26.230 --> 02:27.565
Respect their offering.

15
02:31.819 --> 02:32.778
Save it from the waves!

16
02:33.362 --> 02:35.030
Take it to Athena's temple.

17
02:35.865 --> 02:37.324
The war is over.

18
02:44.623 --> 02:45.583
A face...

19
02:49.253 --> 02:50.838
...a fleet...

20
02:52.882 --> 02:54.133
...a war...

21
02:57.219 --> 02:58.721
...a man...

22
02:58.804 --> 02:59.805
Stop!

23
03:04.393 --> 03:05.603
Not this song.

24
03:10.357 --> 03:12.651
Mommy's boy!

25
03:12.735 --> 03:14.945
Run, run, little man!

26
03:15.487 --> 03:16.488
Run!

27
03:17.531 --> 03:19.909
I wanna remind them of whose wine they're drinking.

28
03:19.992 --> 03:21.785
I don't want Odysseus' song.

29
03:21.869 --> 03:22.953
I want Odysseus.

30
03:23.037 --> 03:24.496
Then why do you entertain suitors

31
03:24.580 --> 03:26.040
in his house night after night?

32
03:26.790 --> 03:30.961
In this world, a man does what he chooses.

33
03:31.045 --> 03:32.129
I do what I can.

34
03:32.213 --> 03:34.924
I am a man now, Mom.

35
03:35.007 --> 03:36.926
So throw them out.

36
03:37.760 --> 03:39.511
Break Zeus' Law and see what they do to you

37
03:39.595 --> 03:41.055
if you give them that excuse.

38
03:46.852 --> 03:49.271
I've already lost a husband.

39
03:49.355 --> 03:51.065
I don't wanna lose a son.

40
03:51.148 --> 03:52.316
But Dad's coming back.

41
03:52.399 --> 03:53.901
Then where is he?

42
03:53.984 --> 03:56.946
Eight years since Troy fell.

43
03:57.029 --> 03:58.864
Almost 20 since he left us.

44
03:58.948 --> 03:59.990
Where is he?

45
04:11.669 --> 04:14.046
We have to welcome him like we welcomed all of you.

46
04:14.129 --> 04:15.422
Are you calling us beggars?

47
04:15.506 --> 04:18.050
I'm saying you've been welcome here for three years.

48
04:18.133 --> 04:21.220
We've only enjoyed your hospitality for so long,

49
04:22.930 --> 04:24.556
because your mother won't choose.

50
04:25.057 --> 04:26.267
She's already married.

51
04:26.350 --> 04:27.810
Odysseus is dead.

52
04:28.477 --> 04:30.229
What do you know about my dad?

53
04:32.690 --> 04:34.066
I know he kept stringy cattle

54
04:34.149 --> 04:35.651
and bad wine.

55
04:35.734 --> 04:38.279
You had the best of it the first two seasons.

56
04:38.362 --> 04:39.947
And I know he never came home.

57
04:40.030 --> 04:41.323
Not yet.

58
04:42.908 --> 04:46.537
You're pining for a daddy you didn't even know.

59
04:46.620 --> 04:48.622
Like some sniveling bastard.

60
04:48.706 --> 04:51.709
Well, since none of us can really know who our fathers are,

61
04:51.792 --> 04:53.377
don't claim to know mine.

62
04:53.460 --> 04:54.586
But I did know him.

63
04:54.670 --> 04:57.423
As boys. We watched him hunt.

64
04:59.508 --> 05:01.635
I tried to volunteer for his expedition to Troy,

65
05:01.719 --> 05:03.220
but he told me to stay here

66
05:03.304 --> 05:04.596
and keep an eye on you.

67
05:04.680 --> 05:06.557
On me, or my mother?

68
05:08.017 --> 05:09.643
You can't blame me for looking.

69
05:09.727 --> 05:11.020
My dad is coming home.

70
05:11.103 --> 05:13.939
And his hospitality won't be as generous as hers.

71
05:14.023 --> 05:16.025
So he doesn't follow Zeus' Law, then?

72
05:16.108 --> 05:19.028
Zeus' Law tells us to treat strangers as we'd be treated

73
05:19.111 --> 05:21.280
because they might be gods in disguise.

74
05:21.905 --> 05:24.533
I think we've established, Antinous, that you are not a god.

75
05:25.826 --> 05:27.786
This one certainly doesn't smell like a god.

76
05:27.870 --> 05:29.496
Wouldn't be much of a disguise if he did.

77
05:29.580 --> 05:30.956
Careful.

78
05:31.457 --> 05:33.709
He might strike you down. Right now.

79
05:39.631 --> 05:40.966
Come on.

80
05:47.473 --> 05:49.308
That thunder was luck.

81
05:50.059 --> 05:52.936
I don't know. You have wise eyes.

82
05:53.979 --> 05:55.481
Athena's eyes. Here.

83
05:55.564 --> 05:57.441
You sailed with traders?

84
05:57.524 --> 05:58.942
From Sparta.

85
05:59.026 --> 06:00.903
Has Menelaus come home from the war?

86
06:01.987 --> 06:04.990
Years ago. Odysseus never came back?

87
06:05.074 --> 06:06.283
We don't even know if he's alive.

88
06:06.367 --> 06:07.409
Have you heard anything?

89
06:07.493 --> 06:09.286
The song of how he won the war.

90
06:09.370 --> 06:10.954
We've all heard that. Anything else?

91
06:11.455 --> 06:12.498
No.

92
06:13.207 --> 06:17.795
But Sparta is full of Trojan War veterans.

93
06:17.878 --> 06:19.797
Someone'll know something.

94
06:19.880 --> 06:23.467
You're Odysseus' son? Go to Sparta.

95
06:23.550 --> 06:26.053
Go. See Menelaus.

96
06:26.720 --> 06:27.721
He'd welcome you.

97
06:29.848 --> 06:31.934
The Queen wants to speak to the stranger.

98
06:33.435 --> 06:35.062
She lives for news.

99
06:37.981 --> 06:39.483
Just don't try to give her any false hope.

100
06:39.566 --> 06:40.818
She has some way of knowing

101
06:40.901 --> 06:43.278
whether a person has actually seen Odysseus.

102
06:43.362 --> 06:44.530
You see that man?

103
06:44.613 --> 06:46.698
He can't see much anymore, but that doesn't stop him

104
06:46.782 --> 06:48.450
beating travelers to death

105
06:48.534 --> 06:50.661
in exchange for lies about my father.

106
07:17.479 --> 07:19.815
No, no, too fast.

107
07:20.441 --> 07:21.525
How can you know?

108
07:21.608 --> 07:25.112
You don't need eyes to hear a hasty combination.

109
07:25.195 --> 07:26.655
Mentor, push him.

110
07:34.830 --> 07:36.039
No, no. Too fast.

111
07:45.716 --> 07:47.050
He's as friendly as you.

112
07:47.134 --> 07:49.887
He only answered to your father.

113
07:49.970 --> 07:51.555
Where'd he get him from?

114
07:51.638 --> 07:53.557
They met right here.

115
07:58.562 --> 08:00.981
Come on.

116
08:01.064 --> 08:02.065
Aw.

117
08:02.733 --> 08:03.984
I'll take him.

118
08:04.067 --> 08:05.068
For your boy?

119
08:05.152 --> 08:06.487
For a hunting dog.

120
08:08.322 --> 08:09.573
Damned if he wasn't right.

121
08:45.150 --> 08:47.945
Hold still. How bad a scar do you want?

122
08:49.613 --> 08:51.448
Why do you always pluck your bow?

123
08:51.532 --> 08:52.533
It's only fair.

124
08:52.616 --> 08:54.409
To warn your prey?

125
08:54.493 --> 08:55.577
To fight with honor.

126
08:55.661 --> 08:57.538
But you weren't fighting. You were hunting.

127
08:57.621 --> 08:59.540
Ah, hunting's nothing but fighting with stealth.

128
09:00.040 --> 09:01.291
Clever.

129
09:01.375 --> 09:04.044
Clever, but your cleverness gonna get you in trouble.

130
09:04.127 --> 09:05.087
Try again.

131
09:05.796 --> 09:08.048
This time, not as fast or precise.

132
09:08.131 --> 09:10.676
You can't win with a perfect defense.

133
09:10.759 --> 09:11.885
Draw the attack.

134
09:11.969 --> 09:13.845
- How? - Hesitate.

135
09:13.929 --> 09:16.515
Open your belly to your opponent's blade.

136
09:16.598 --> 09:18.267
Their attack becomes your chance.

137
09:50.173 --> 09:51.633
You teach my father that?

138
09:51.717 --> 09:54.303
No, no, he taught me.

139
09:54.386 --> 09:56.138
He said, "You're not always

140
09:56.221 --> 09:58.515
going to be the strongest or the fastest,

141
09:58.599 --> 10:01.643
so be... Be the smartest."

142
10:01.727 --> 10:03.145
What would he do, huh?

143
10:03.228 --> 10:05.647
If he came back here to find all these suitors in his house?

144
10:05.731 --> 10:09.151
Don't. I can't even let myself imagine what he'd do to them.

145
10:09.234 --> 10:10.777
There are so many.

146
10:10.861 --> 10:13.030
Cut a couple down, the others cave.

147
10:13.113 --> 10:15.073
Bullies and cowards.

148
10:15.157 --> 10:16.783
Most of them were old enough to go to Troy,

149
10:16.867 --> 10:18.493
but when they saw Agamemnon's sail...

150
10:22.331 --> 10:23.707
...their fathers hid them.

151
10:27.336 --> 10:28.837
And he knew Agamemnon's war

152
10:28.920 --> 10:31.256
would take many years and many lives.

153
10:36.261 --> 10:38.555
So he raised his army by lottery.

154
10:41.433 --> 10:42.476
- Name? - Sinon.

155
10:42.559 --> 10:44.353
Draw. No.

156
10:44.436 --> 10:45.687
- Name? - Antinous.

157
10:45.771 --> 10:46.813
Draw.

158
10:48.398 --> 10:49.316
Yes.

159
10:49.399 --> 10:50.567
By lots?

160
10:53.236 --> 10:55.405
Antinous told me he volunteered.

161
10:55.489 --> 10:57.074
I think they're up to something.

162
11:05.540 --> 11:07.501
Your boy's trying to take Sinon's place.

163
11:08.502 --> 11:09.920
I'm not gonna let him.

164
11:10.003 --> 11:11.838
He needs to stay here. Take care of his family.

165
11:14.883 --> 11:16.259
He can watch over your boy too.

166
11:16.343 --> 11:17.594
Of course.

167
11:21.515 --> 11:22.599
He's with me.

168
11:23.100 --> 11:25.310
He wanted Antinous to look after me?

169
11:25.394 --> 11:26.853
No, no, no.

170
11:26.937 --> 11:29.648
But Odysseus was sensitive to a father's shame.

171
11:29.731 --> 11:31.400
He felt for these fathers.

172
11:31.483 --> 11:34.569
You and your mother meant more to him than promises of glory.

173
11:36.113 --> 11:37.280
So he knew it would be years.

174
11:38.907 --> 11:40.617
Did he tell my mother?

175
11:40.701 --> 11:42.119
That's a question for her.

176
11:42.202 --> 11:45.414
Eumaeus, is he ever coming back?

177
11:46.790 --> 11:48.083
Argus thinks so.

178
11:48.166 --> 11:50.335
Never known a dog reach 20.

179
11:51.670 --> 11:52.963
But he's long outlived his master.

180
12:10.272 --> 12:11.690
I didn't mean to stop you.

181
12:14.901 --> 12:16.361
I'm in no hurry.

182
12:17.446 --> 12:20.741
The elders decreed when I finish your grandfather's burial shroud,

183
12:20.824 --> 12:22.200
it'll be time to remarry.

184
12:22.284 --> 12:23.326
Why?

185
12:29.040 --> 12:31.001
Ithaca's been without a king too long.

186
12:31.835 --> 12:33.253
They want security.

187
12:33.336 --> 12:34.337
From what?

188
12:34.421 --> 12:36.381
The people from the sea.

189
12:36.465 --> 12:38.717
That's just traveler's tales.

190
12:38.800 --> 12:40.635
All we know of the world is traveler's tales.

191
12:40.719 --> 12:42.512
That's how we found out Troy had fallen.

192
12:43.096 --> 12:44.347
Now they all tell stories

193
12:44.431 --> 12:46.475
of attacks by people from the sea.

194
12:47.559 --> 12:49.436
And who are the people from the sea?

195
12:49.519 --> 12:52.647
No one knows, but the elders want Ithaca to be prepared.

196
12:52.731 --> 12:53.774
Prepared?

197
12:53.857 --> 12:56.610
We're part of the greatest civilization ever known.

198
12:58.445 --> 13:00.071
Look at this great house.

199
13:00.155 --> 13:01.907
Stones as old as knowledge.

200
13:01.990 --> 13:05.702
At the mercy of some greedy, unwanted guests?

201
13:05.786 --> 13:07.788
The structure's nothing

202
13:08.705 --> 13:10.332
without people's respect for its meaning.

203
13:11.082 --> 13:12.083
Zeus' Law.

204
13:13.084 --> 13:15.086
Treat others as you would be treated.

205
13:15.170 --> 13:18.173
They abuse that, they destroy everything.

206
13:18.256 --> 13:20.926
So how could you think of marrying one of them?

207
13:21.009 --> 13:23.428
Because, a long time ago,

208
13:24.763 --> 13:26.890
your father asked me to.

209
13:26.973 --> 13:28.475
Agamemnon is our king.

210
13:28.558 --> 13:31.102
And the Trojans took his brother's wife.

211
13:31.186 --> 13:33.230
Or she ran away with them.

212
13:33.313 --> 13:36.858
Either way. The walls of Troy are said to be impregnable.

213
13:37.943 --> 13:40.445
And we'll sit outside that great city,

214
13:40.529 --> 13:42.322
trying to draw them into battle.

215
13:44.366 --> 13:47.327
Then years go by. And Telemachus grows.

216
13:47.953 --> 13:50.080
And you learn life without me.

217
13:50.163 --> 13:52.874
Menelaus will give up on Helen.

218
13:57.295 --> 13:59.172
This is Agamemnon's excuse

219
13:59.256 --> 14:02.509
to break Troy's control of the trading routes.

220
14:04.469 --> 14:07.764
He won't let it pass. Ever.

221
14:14.479 --> 14:16.731
Well, what if you refuse?

222
14:22.487 --> 14:24.155
He'll take our son.

223
14:25.699 --> 14:27.117
He would never.

224
14:27.200 --> 14:29.327
He sacrificed his own daughter.

225
14:29.411 --> 14:31.204
For favorable winds.

226
14:32.497 --> 14:34.875
Why would the gods want his daughter's life?

227
14:35.500 --> 14:37.377
The power of a sacrifice

228
14:38.837 --> 14:41.464
is in the cost to the person making it.

229
14:44.801 --> 14:46.636
That's monstrous.

230
14:49.055 --> 14:50.640
And committed.

231
14:53.476 --> 14:55.312
I won't return soon.

232
14:56.271 --> 14:57.606
If at all.

233
15:01.943 --> 15:03.612
What if we ran?

234
15:05.071 --> 15:07.073
Took your fastest ship.

235
15:07.157 --> 15:08.366
And brightest crew.

236
15:08.450 --> 15:10.619
And headed for the horizon.

237
15:12.203 --> 15:16.791
Held hands on deck as we rowed west at dusk,

238
15:16.875 --> 15:18.710
chasing the escaping sun.

239
15:22.339 --> 15:24.132
There's a world beyond these walls.

240
15:25.050 --> 15:30.472
Agamemnon's vengeance will fall on all of Ithaca.

241
15:36.603 --> 15:37.854
Now promise me.

242
15:40.148 --> 15:42.233
If I don't return

243
15:42.317 --> 15:44.319
by the time Telemachus comes of age,

244
15:45.070 --> 15:46.404
promise me

245
15:47.656 --> 15:50.116
you'll take another husband.

246
15:51.660 --> 15:53.203
Enough sacrifices.

247
15:53.286 --> 15:55.038
Love's not a sacrifice.

248
15:55.121 --> 15:56.331
Of course it is.

249
15:57.624 --> 15:59.000
I don't want you to make it.

250
15:59.084 --> 16:01.920
It's not up to you.

251
16:02.837 --> 16:04.089
Promise me.

252
16:05.674 --> 16:06.675
No.

253
16:10.512 --> 16:12.305
Promise me you'll come back.

254
16:17.852 --> 16:19.145
What if I can't?

255
16:25.110 --> 16:27.904
Then your memory won't be making my decisions.

256
16:32.784 --> 16:34.744
Take my pin.

257
16:34.828 --> 16:35.996
Wear it always.

258
16:36.079 --> 16:38.581
Athena will bring you home.

259
16:38.665 --> 16:40.125
I'm not sure it suits me.

260
16:42.711 --> 16:45.296
When travelers claim to have seen you,

261
16:45.380 --> 16:47.382
I'll ask what you were wearing.

262
16:47.465 --> 16:49.092
And I'll know if they were lying.

263
16:52.429 --> 16:54.514
If the people from the sea are real,

264
16:55.724 --> 16:59.352
right now, Ithaca has no king to raise an army.

265
16:59.936 --> 17:01.438
I could take the throne.

266
17:03.648 --> 17:04.816
Come.

267
17:16.619 --> 17:17.829
String it.

268
17:28.131 --> 17:29.841
That's not fair.

269
17:29.924 --> 17:31.926
You said only he could string this bow.

270
17:32.010 --> 17:33.803
He was happy to let others try.

271
17:36.222 --> 17:37.891
He'd line up all the axes.

272
17:39.434 --> 17:40.643
Yeah.

273
18:10.423 --> 18:11.925
No one else ever did it.

274
18:13.301 --> 18:16.346
Mom, they're bleeding us dry so you'll be forced to choose one of them

275
18:16.429 --> 18:19.057
before I can claim the throne myself.

276
18:19.140 --> 18:21.518
The traveler told me Menelaus returned years ago.

277
18:21.601 --> 18:23.019
They've all returned.

278
18:23.103 --> 18:24.687
All who survived.

279
18:26.189 --> 18:27.649
Maybe he is dead.

280
18:28.149 --> 18:30.235
And if he is, then this is my palace.

281
18:31.361 --> 18:32.362
We need to know.

282
18:32.445 --> 18:35.198
I have to go to Menelaus to find out the truth.

283
18:35.281 --> 18:36.574
Telemachus,

284
18:37.408 --> 18:38.827
these suitors mean you harm.

285
18:38.910 --> 18:41.287
You think I don't know that?

286
18:41.371 --> 18:43.915
Every night, they push me to give them the excuse they need.

287
18:43.998 --> 18:47.168
And out there, on the sea, on the road?

288
18:47.836 --> 18:50.004
They won't need an excuse. You just won't come back.

289
18:52.340 --> 18:54.467
You don't want me to claim the throne myself.

290
18:56.469 --> 19:00.306
You just wanna keep me in my place so that you can stay Queen of Ithaca.

291
19:00.390 --> 19:03.101
I've had enough of being Queen of Ithaca without my king!

292
19:03.184 --> 19:05.061
Without your king?

293
19:06.146 --> 19:07.147
Or "a" king?

294
19:08.815 --> 19:11.818
And you think you're ready to take your father's place?

295
19:13.653 --> 19:14.863
You're a child.

296
19:25.331 --> 19:27.208
Too loud, Melanthius.

297
19:27.292 --> 19:29.043
Don't wear your spurs in the palace.

298
19:29.127 --> 19:31.129
You're a cowherd, not a horseman.

299
19:31.212 --> 19:32.630
And you're a pig farmer.

300
19:32.714 --> 19:35.091
And who are you to talk to me like that? Hmm?

301
19:35.175 --> 19:38.553
The servant our master left in charge of his herdsmen.

302
19:39.053 --> 19:42.682
Our master is long gone, you blind old fool.

303
19:42.765 --> 19:45.226
And you're about as welcome here as that dog is.

304
19:46.394 --> 19:47.437
Argus!

305
19:51.107 --> 19:52.150
Argus!

306
19:53.484 --> 19:54.861
Argus.

307
19:54.944 --> 19:56.154
Argus.

308
19:57.572 --> 19:59.157
Sir, he didn't do anything.

309
19:59.240 --> 20:00.658
So that stink is you?

310
20:01.910 --> 20:03.244
Leave the old dog alone.

311
20:03.328 --> 20:05.205
- Which one? - Both of us.

312
20:06.456 --> 20:07.498
What did you say to me, slave?

313
20:07.582 --> 20:08.583
He just got carried away.

314
20:08.666 --> 20:10.501
Argus is my father's hunting dog.

315
20:10.585 --> 20:11.878
His hunting dog?

316
20:16.007 --> 20:17.300
Look at that thing.

317
20:18.343 --> 20:19.969
Get it out of here. We're trying to eat.

318
20:20.053 --> 20:22.055
Don't! Can't you see he's in pain?

319
20:22.138 --> 20:24.474
Okay. Then I'll put it out of its misery.

320
20:24.974 --> 20:26.517
Leave him alone! Argh!

321
20:43.826 --> 20:44.827
Did you see?

322
20:45.828 --> 20:47.163
You all see?

323
20:48.665 --> 20:49.916
He attacked me!

324
20:50.500 --> 20:51.501
I have the right!

325
20:52.252 --> 20:53.878
Please!

326
20:55.797 --> 20:57.382
Please don't hurt my son.

327
21:00.593 --> 21:02.470
Easy. Easy. Polybus.

328
21:03.054 --> 21:05.181
The young pup lost his head. Show some mercy.

329
21:05.932 --> 21:08.851
Here. Drink some more of his wine.

330
21:08.935 --> 21:10.311
I'll take care of it.

331
21:10.895 --> 21:12.814
It's my father's hunting dog.

332
21:13.856 --> 21:15.817
He's only living to see Odysseus return.

333
21:15.900 --> 21:16.901
It's fine.

334
21:16.985 --> 21:18.611
I'll put him on the dung heap out front

335
21:18.695 --> 21:20.905
so he can greet his master a little sooner.

336
21:31.040 --> 21:32.917
That dog stays out there.

337
21:34.669 --> 21:36.254
Next time, think twice before you offer up your life

338
21:36.337 --> 21:37.672
for an animal.

339
21:49.142 --> 21:50.143
Eurycleia.

340
21:52.061 --> 21:53.855
Prepare supplies. Take them down to the ships.

341
21:53.938 --> 21:54.981
Tell no one.

342
21:55.064 --> 21:56.399
Use only people you trust.

343
21:56.482 --> 21:57.984
You'll break your mother's heart.

344
21:58.067 --> 22:00.028
If I stay, they'll force me to fight.

345
22:00.111 --> 22:01.696
No one will back me.

346
22:01.779 --> 22:03.573
I have to find out what happened to my father.

347
22:03.656 --> 22:07.285
Ithaca will be behind me if they know Odysseus is dead.

348
22:07.368 --> 22:08.870
The suitors will never allow that.

349
22:08.953 --> 22:10.038
You will never make it back alive.

350
22:10.121 --> 22:14.000
Tell Mentor to meet me at the ships just before dawn.

351
22:14.083 --> 22:16.794
What do you want me to tell your mother?

352
22:17.795 --> 22:19.547
To hold out for as long as she can.

353
22:23.217 --> 22:25.178
Ship's ready. Tide's right.

354
22:25.928 --> 22:28.348
Have I ever told you that you have wise eyes?

355
22:28.431 --> 22:29.807
Athena's eyes?

356
22:29.891 --> 22:30.892
Once or twice.

357
22:30.975 --> 22:32.935
You haven't even asked me where we're going yet.

358
22:33.019 --> 22:34.812
Where are we going?

359
22:34.896 --> 22:35.897
To find my dad.

360
22:36.606 --> 22:38.983
He's alive. I can feel it in the wind and the waves.

361
22:39.067 --> 22:40.276
He's out there somewhere,

362
22:40.360 --> 22:42.528
wandering, injured, lost, I don't know.

363
22:43.988 --> 22:45.615
But he's alive.

364
23:16.854 --> 23:18.606
More dreams?

365
23:19.399 --> 23:21.025
Men drowning.

366
23:21.109 --> 23:22.151
Inside.

367
23:23.444 --> 23:27.240
Some dark space.

368
23:29.575 --> 23:32.120
Firelight on stone. I don't know.

369
23:34.956 --> 23:35.957
Don't you wanna remember?

370
23:36.582 --> 23:39.293
Why can't gods speak in ways we understand?

371
23:42.004 --> 23:43.798
Who doesn't understand thunder?

372
23:44.590 --> 23:45.716
Or fire?

373
23:46.217 --> 23:48.928
A child's smile, a good harvest?

374
23:49.011 --> 23:51.097
But you hide yourselves from us.

375
23:52.807 --> 23:53.808
Look.

376
23:54.684 --> 23:56.102
Who's moving that cloud?

377
23:57.061 --> 23:58.604
Amassing those birds?

378
24:00.022 --> 24:01.441
Who's moving the water?

379
24:03.484 --> 24:04.777
Who were you talking to?

380
24:07.697 --> 24:09.115
Athena came to me again.

381
24:09.907 --> 24:11.701
She's telling me to remember.

382
24:13.411 --> 24:14.495
To leave.

383
24:14.579 --> 24:15.580
Why would you leave?

384
24:16.205 --> 24:18.291
We have everything we need.

385
24:18.875 --> 24:20.793
How long have I been here, Calypso?

386
24:25.214 --> 24:27.008
I have a home somewhere.

387
24:28.843 --> 24:29.927
There's someone.

388
24:30.553 --> 24:31.679
Out there.

389
24:32.388 --> 24:33.556
How do you know?

390
24:34.056 --> 24:35.266
I don't know.

391
24:37.852 --> 24:38.853
I feel.

392
24:55.036 --> 24:56.954
What we have here could last forever.

393
24:57.038 --> 24:58.372
Forever is for gods.

394
24:58.456 --> 24:59.707
Are you a god?

395
25:02.710 --> 25:03.711
You wanna remember,

396
25:03.794 --> 25:06.297
but what if remembering destroys your happiness?

397
25:06.380 --> 25:07.882
Then it wasn't real.

398
25:15.932 --> 25:16.974
No.

399
25:17.058 --> 25:18.559
- No more. - But I love it.

400
25:19.060 --> 25:20.937
Stop eating the lotus.

401
25:21.020 --> 25:23.022
Tell me what you remember.

402
25:24.649 --> 25:25.650
After Troy.

403
25:27.818 --> 25:28.819
Troy.

404
25:35.201 --> 25:36.494
We won the war.

405
25:37.078 --> 25:38.371
And then what?

406
26:44.020 --> 26:45.646
Ten years on this fucking beach.

407
26:51.193 --> 26:52.361
Let's go home.

408
26:53.779 --> 26:55.656
Remember who we leave here.

409
27:00.286 --> 27:01.787
Think we have enough provisions?

410
27:02.580 --> 27:04.165
We've taken enough from Troy.

411
27:05.166 --> 27:06.709
Another stop or two won't kill us.

412
27:08.419 --> 27:10.546
And... pull!

413
27:11.422 --> 27:13.674
And... pull!

414
27:14.592 --> 27:16.886
And... pull!

415
27:17.803 --> 27:18.971
And...

416
27:19.055 --> 27:20.264
Stow the oars.

417
27:20.348 --> 27:21.349
Rest!

418
27:21.432 --> 27:22.642
Rest!

419
27:22.725 --> 27:23.768
Stow the oars!

420
27:23.851 --> 27:25.436
Stow the oars!

421
27:30.358 --> 27:31.525
We'll raise the sail,

422
27:31.609 --> 27:32.610
follow the southerly winds.

423
27:32.693 --> 27:33.694
Yes, sir.

424
27:34.737 --> 27:37.114
Agamemnon's rowing west on the known provisions route.

425
27:37.198 --> 27:38.616
Well, he's got more mouths to feed.

426
27:39.659 --> 27:40.993
We'll find places to land.

427
27:41.077 --> 27:42.370
Are you sure? It's a risk.

428
27:42.453 --> 27:44.205
I followed Agamemnon long enough.

429
27:45.122 --> 27:47.541
With a little luck and the right winds,

430
27:47.625 --> 27:49.377
we'll see some of the world,

431
27:49.460 --> 27:51.253
still be home before Agamemnon.

432
27:54.757 --> 27:55.800
Steady, boys!

433
29:01.115 --> 29:02.742
Just keep the bear on the right.

434
29:12.418 --> 29:13.419
Odysseus!

435
29:13.502 --> 29:14.879
Land!

436
29:18.090 --> 29:19.258
See?

437
29:19.341 --> 29:21.844
Lucky bastard.

438
29:21.927 --> 29:23.512
We're down to our last supplies.

439
29:23.596 --> 29:25.139
Assemble a landing party.

440
29:25.222 --> 29:27.475
Food and water. Enough for three weeks.

441
29:27.558 --> 29:28.768
What if they won't give it?

442
29:30.811 --> 29:32.688
How badly do you wanna get home?

443
29:33.314 --> 29:35.232
Get your weapons ready!

444
29:35.316 --> 29:36.484
- Begging... - Move it!

445
29:36.567 --> 29:37.610
Borrowing...

446
29:38.486 --> 29:39.570
Plundering.

447
29:43.365 --> 29:46.410
Ask him why they abandoned their village instead of welcoming us?

448
29:53.459 --> 29:55.127
He says they thought we were the sea people.

449
29:55.211 --> 29:56.253
No.

450
29:56.337 --> 29:58.756
We're Greeks. From Ithaca.

451
29:59.673 --> 30:01.425
Maybe you heard of our victory at Troy.

452
30:02.176 --> 30:04.386
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

453
30:11.018 --> 30:12.436
He says if we go that way,

454
30:13.187 --> 30:14.897
we won't like the things we'll find.

455
30:16.315 --> 30:18.025
After years of war,

456
30:19.026 --> 30:22.905
no one could stand between my men and home.

457
30:23.823 --> 30:25.074
Not even me.

458
30:26.408 --> 30:28.202
Then something happened.

459
30:43.968 --> 30:45.845
Chasing the escaping sun.

460
30:46.554 --> 30:47.721
Land!

461
30:49.265 --> 30:50.599
Land!

462
30:50.683 --> 30:51.684
There you go.

463
30:55.020 --> 30:56.730
Lucky bastard.

464
31:08.826 --> 31:10.494
No, no, no.

465
31:10.578 --> 31:12.913
Sheep come in flocks.

466
31:14.164 --> 31:15.165
Let's follow.

467
32:35.829 --> 32:37.414
Someone lives here.

468
32:38.707 --> 32:39.875
Yeah, they make cheese.

469
32:58.727 --> 32:59.895
Pretty good.

470
33:02.731 --> 33:03.983
There's a lot of it.

471
33:04.066 --> 33:05.275
We should leave.

472
33:06.026 --> 33:07.569
We brought nothing to trade with.

473
33:08.237 --> 33:09.655
Zeus' Law.

474
33:09.738 --> 33:11.824
I don't know if whoever lives here has heard of Zeus' Law.

475
33:11.907 --> 33:13.242
Or Zeus for that matter.

476
33:13.325 --> 33:15.119
Everyone's heard of Zeus' Law.

477
33:15.619 --> 33:16.829
Get the fire going!

478
33:16.912 --> 33:18.580
We'll welcome the owner back.

479
34:37.951 --> 34:39.119
Get away from the fire.

480
34:43.957 --> 34:45.542
Spread out!

481
36:18.510 --> 36:19.928
I think it's asleep.

482
37:05.265 --> 37:06.516
Wait.

483
37:09.519 --> 37:10.646
The rock.

484
37:18.278 --> 37:19.279
We can't move the rock.

485
37:19.363 --> 37:20.739
We'd be trapped.

486
37:29.206 --> 37:30.207
Spread the word.

487
37:30.290 --> 37:33.794
In the morning, when it lets out its flock,

488
37:33.877 --> 37:35.337
we'll rush the entrance.

489
37:35.420 --> 37:36.546
Rest up.

490
38:01.530 --> 38:03.198
Go, go, go!

491
38:13.542 --> 38:14.918
Go!

492
38:23.218 --> 38:26.013
Could we jam something in the cave mouth?

493
38:26.096 --> 38:27.306
Leave a gap?

494
38:28.015 --> 38:29.016
Maybe.

495
38:30.058 --> 38:31.059
But what?

496
38:31.560 --> 38:32.686
This log.

497
38:36.356 --> 38:37.691
It'll notice.

498
38:39.276 --> 38:41.903
But grab the thin end. Put it in the fire.

499
38:42.779 --> 38:43.822
How much rope do we have?

500
38:44.948 --> 38:47.159
- How big's the flock? - Couple dozen, maybe.

501
38:49.870 --> 38:51.163
There's too many of us.

502
38:51.663 --> 38:54.082
When it comes back, two of us have to get out.

503
38:54.166 --> 38:55.667
Or at least get killed.

504
39:00.797 --> 39:02.758
Run low through the flock towards the entrance,

505
39:02.841 --> 39:03.925
then split wide.

506
39:04.009 --> 39:06.720
- So he can only grab one of 'em. - Right.

507
39:06.803 --> 39:09.306
Four of us should make the run. Two and two.

508
39:09.389 --> 39:10.932
Why risk more men?

509
39:11.016 --> 39:12.476
It ate two of us last night.

510
39:12.559 --> 39:14.353
He'll want another two before he sleeps.

511
39:39.211 --> 39:40.253
Go, go, go!

512
41:18.810 --> 41:21.188
- What about his body? - What about it?

513
41:21.271 --> 41:23.106
We just leave him here to rot?

514
41:24.399 --> 41:25.859
As food for that thing?

515
41:27.694 --> 41:30.405
We honor our dead, Odysseus.

516
41:30.489 --> 41:33.366
He died helping us escape. We'll honor him by escaping.

517
41:36.786 --> 41:39.873
Father Poseidon, they blinded me.

518
41:40.790 --> 41:44.336
Vengeance, Father. Vengeance for pain.

519
41:44.419 --> 41:47.714
Poseidon, vengeance for pain.

520
41:47.797 --> 41:49.674
He can talk.

521
41:50.342 --> 41:51.676
Why didn't it talk before?

522
41:53.428 --> 41:54.888
Do you talk to ants?

523
41:56.431 --> 41:57.974
Should we reason with it?

524
42:00.477 --> 42:01.811
I think we're past that.

525
43:13.091 --> 43:14.426
What are you doing?

526
43:15.343 --> 43:17.512
No!

527
43:33.945 --> 43:36.156
Come on. Go, go!

528
43:36.239 --> 43:38.074
Go!

529
44:06.144 --> 44:07.145
Get in!

530
44:09.356 --> 44:10.357
Go!

531
44:31.670 --> 44:33.129
- Go, go, go! - Go, go, go!

532
44:34.422 --> 44:37.092
Go! Go!

533
44:57.737 --> 44:59.531
Why'd you have to fire that arrow?

534
44:59.614 --> 45:01.783
We lost good soldiers to that beast.

535
45:01.866 --> 45:04.911
Yeah, and that beast was the son of Poseidon.

536
45:04.994 --> 45:06.079
What?

537
45:06.162 --> 45:08.707
When we put its eye out, we heard it crying his name.

538
45:08.790 --> 45:10.500
That thing was a monster.

539
45:10.583 --> 45:12.419
I heard it too.

540
45:12.502 --> 45:14.504
So now the sea's against us?

541
45:14.587 --> 45:16.548
And the wind.

542
45:16.631 --> 45:19.217
How are we supposed to get home with Poseidon against us?

543
45:19.300 --> 45:21.803
We can't live by omens and sacrifices.

544
45:24.055 --> 45:26.057
Do you know how many times I've heard you weeping,

545
45:26.141 --> 45:28.226
praying to Athena since we left Troy?

546
45:28.309 --> 45:29.769
Couldn't you have shown some mercy?

547
45:31.229 --> 45:32.230
Take this.

548
45:34.816 --> 45:35.942
Wear it.

549
45:36.025 --> 45:38.278
To remember those we couldn't bury.

550
45:40.029 --> 45:41.865
The gods help those who help themselves.

551
45:45.618 --> 45:49.289
From that moment, every rough sea or headwind

552
45:49.539 --> 45:51.124
told the crew we were cursed.

553
45:53.543 --> 45:55.128
But where is my crew?

554
45:56.755 --> 45:58.673
When will you let me see my men?

555
45:59.674 --> 46:02.218
If I could just talk to them they'd help me remember.

556
46:02.302 --> 46:03.303
Did I have a wife?

557
46:03.386 --> 46:04.721
Children? Maybe a son.

558
46:04.804 --> 46:06.806
How long have I been here?

559
46:08.433 --> 46:09.476
A long time.

560
46:10.059 --> 46:11.060
If I had a son,

561
46:13.730 --> 46:15.231
how old would he be now?

562
46:17.275 --> 46:19.277
I don't know how to speak to a king.

563
46:19.360 --> 46:20.695
You'll do fine.

564
46:20.779 --> 46:22.071
Just keep your mouth shut.

565
46:22.864 --> 46:26.326
But, if anyone asks, we're traders from Crete.

566
46:26.409 --> 46:28.536
Then we'll figure out how to approach the king.

567
46:38.421 --> 46:39.672
Welcome.

568
46:39.756 --> 46:41.800
Thank you. You seem to be having quite...

569
46:41.883 --> 46:43.092
This way.

570
46:48.264 --> 46:49.516
Where are you taking us?

571
46:49.599 --> 46:52.268
The king insists on meeting strangers himself.

572
47:13.623 --> 47:14.624
Sit.

573
47:18.169 --> 47:19.170
Not you.

574
47:20.547 --> 47:21.548
You.

575
47:23.967 --> 47:25.885
Enjoy our hospitality.

576
47:25.969 --> 47:27.428
As Zeus demands.

577
47:27.971 --> 47:29.806
Then, perhaps, you'll tell us who you are.

578
47:31.182 --> 47:32.809
You picked the right day to arrive.

579
47:32.892 --> 47:35.019
Feast of my daughter's wedding.

580
47:35.103 --> 47:37.605
Tomorrow she leaves to start a new life.

581
47:38.398 --> 47:40.483
You don't look like a beggar.

582
47:40.567 --> 47:41.693
Imagine you know who I am.

583
47:43.778 --> 47:46.614
I'm Menelaus. The son of Atreus.

584
47:47.115 --> 47:48.867
Brother of Agamemnon.

585
47:49.701 --> 47:51.286
The husband of Helen.

586
47:51.870 --> 47:52.912
Perhaps you've heard of Helen.

587
47:55.957 --> 47:59.586
Face that launched a thousand ships.

588
48:01.379 --> 48:03.256
Though maybe now just 500.

589
48:07.886 --> 48:10.889
So many left on the battlefields of Troy for that face.

590
48:10.972 --> 48:13.182
For your brother's ambition.

591
48:14.100 --> 48:15.935
This one looks familiar.

592
48:17.353 --> 48:18.438
Ajax...

593
48:19.063 --> 48:20.106
Patroclus...

594
48:20.189 --> 48:21.566
Achilles...

595
48:22.108 --> 48:25.653
I don't miss them as much as one particular warrior.

596
48:26.487 --> 48:28.156
He was the smartest of us all.

597
48:30.617 --> 48:32.911
Ten years we rotted on that beach.

598
48:46.299 --> 48:47.675
Deadlocked.

599
48:47.759 --> 48:49.761
Fighting amongst ourselves until one man...

600
48:53.097 --> 48:54.432
He found a way.

601
48:56.184 --> 48:57.435
Odysseus.

602
48:58.519 --> 48:59.729
You hear the story of the horse?

603
49:03.483 --> 49:04.734
But did you hear it from the inside?

604
49:09.614 --> 49:10.823
We were in there for days.

605
49:12.241 --> 49:14.827
We squeezed inside before the ship set sail.

606
49:16.287 --> 49:18.748
Odysseus, he knew that no one could know that we were inside.

607
49:18.831 --> 49:21.626
Not even Sinon, the soldier we left behind.

608
49:27.090 --> 49:29.968
First two tides, our men drowned right in front of us.

609
49:35.723 --> 49:38.518
We lay silent. In our piss and shit.

610
50:06.838 --> 50:08.006
A gift.

611
50:08.631 --> 50:10.008
For Athena.

612
50:12.343 --> 50:13.511
Common gods.

613
50:14.303 --> 50:15.847
Respect their offering.

614
51:37.386 --> 51:39.514
You should be burned!

615
51:39.597 --> 51:41.808
Burn it! Burn it!

616
52:03.913 --> 52:05.289
It's an offering for Athena!

617
52:14.382 --> 52:16.634
It's an offering for Athena!

618
54:52.623 --> 54:53.666
The gates!

619
54:55.543 --> 54:57.628
He knew to wait for a moonless night

620
54:57.712 --> 54:59.338
on the plains of Troy.

621
55:06.012 --> 55:07.346
Go! Go!

622
55:12.601 --> 55:14.812
- City's under attack! - Get to the gates!

623
55:15.396 --> 55:16.605
To the gates!

624
55:35.624 --> 55:36.917
Go, go!

625
55:37.877 --> 55:39.670
The city's under attack!

626
56:51.409 --> 56:53.369
- Come on! - Go, go!

627
57:26.527 --> 57:28.237
I think you know the rest.

628
57:28.320 --> 57:29.321
You moved by my telling it?

629
57:29.405 --> 57:31.574
You should hear the bards sing it.

630
57:31.657 --> 57:32.783
I have.

631
57:36.203 --> 57:37.746
But they weren't there with all of you.

632
57:37.830 --> 57:39.582
With your father.

633
57:39.665 --> 57:41.000
Odysseus.

634
57:43.752 --> 57:45.004
You're so like him.

635
57:45.880 --> 57:47.465
When did you last see him?

636
57:47.548 --> 57:48.799
Troy.

637
57:49.550 --> 57:51.719
The gods granted us a righteous victory...

638
57:51.802 --> 57:52.803
Righteous.

639
57:53.804 --> 57:57.641
The gods granted our righteous victory but did not bless our voyages.

640
57:58.767 --> 58:00.811
Took me years to get home.

641
58:00.895 --> 58:01.896
But he didn't.

642
58:01.979 --> 58:03.522
Some did and wished they hadn't.

643
58:03.606 --> 58:05.858
Tell him.

644
58:05.941 --> 58:08.611
- Tell him about your brother. - No.

645
58:08.694 --> 58:10.029
About the fate that the gods bestowed on him

646
58:10.112 --> 58:11.614
after your righteous struggle.

647
58:11.697 --> 58:13.365
- No. - About his hero's welcome.

648
58:13.449 --> 58:14.783
- No. - About how Agamemnon

649
58:14.867 --> 58:16.827
- made it home years before us. - No.

650
58:16.911 --> 58:19.038
Tell him how he was welcomed by his queen,

651
58:19.121 --> 58:20.873
my twin sister Clytemnestra.

652
58:20.956 --> 58:22.374
- No. No. - Tell him how she...

653
58:22.458 --> 58:24.877
No! He does not want to hear about my brother!

654
58:28.130 --> 58:29.423
He wants news of his father.

655
58:30.591 --> 58:32.968
I know nothing of Odysseus since Troy.

656
58:33.052 --> 58:34.595
Nothing?

657
58:35.471 --> 58:37.264
Not from any of your men?

658
58:37.932 --> 58:40.434
Or Agamemnon's, or traders, or travelers that told you something?

659
58:40.518 --> 58:43.646
Ah, did you come here for rumors? Is that what you're interested in? Gossip?

660
58:44.355 --> 58:45.731
Well, you're in the right place.

661
58:45.814 --> 58:48.734
Anyone wanna spin a story about Odysseus?

662
58:49.235 --> 58:52.071
Some say he's rich or some say poor.

663
58:53.113 --> 58:55.574
Some say he perished. Some say he's in prison.

664
58:55.658 --> 58:57.201
- What say you? - In prison?

665
59:00.496 --> 59:01.830
What kind of prison

666
59:02.748 --> 59:04.375
could hold a man like that?

667
59:05.584 --> 59:07.753
Tell me what you do remember.

668
59:07.836 --> 59:08.921
The rest will come.

669
59:10.172 --> 59:13.842
The men became more and more convinced that I'd offended Poseidon.

670
59:34.405 --> 59:36.490
- Ready! - Ready!

671
59:37.825 --> 59:39.243
Pull!

672
59:39.827 --> 59:42.830
And... pull!

673
59:44.290 --> 59:46.125
And... pull!

674
59:57.761 --> 59:59.680
Pull! And...

675
59:59.763 --> 01:00:01.640
We were hit by storm after storm.

676
01:00:01.724 --> 01:00:03.892
All pushing us off course.

677
01:00:03.976 --> 01:00:05.978
Until we were truly lost.

678
01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:12.234
And... pull!

679
01:00:12.318 --> 01:00:14.528
And... pull!

680
01:00:16.989 --> 01:00:17.990
Pull!

681
01:00:18.866 --> 01:00:22.620
And... pull!

682
01:00:22.703 --> 01:00:24.955
And... pull!

683
01:00:58.405 --> 01:00:59.490
Hello.

684
01:01:01.408 --> 01:01:02.451
We're here in peace.

685
01:01:05.204 --> 01:01:06.914
We just need some food.

686
01:01:12.836 --> 01:01:14.463
We need food.

687
01:01:15.839 --> 01:01:17.925
- Can you understand that? - It's just a child.

688
01:01:20.511 --> 01:01:23.180
If that's a child, what about its parents?

689
01:01:25.265 --> 01:01:27.184
Hey. Shh! It's okay.

690
01:01:27.851 --> 01:01:30.604
No, no, no.

691
01:01:49.039 --> 01:01:51.083
Go! Go!

692
01:01:51.166 --> 01:01:53.460
Back to the ship! Go!

693
01:02:08.058 --> 01:02:09.226
Go, go. Go.

694
01:02:18.193 --> 01:02:20.195
Fall back! Fall back! Go! Go!

695
01:03:30.182 --> 01:03:31.767
Run! Faster!

696
01:03:41.068 --> 01:03:43.362
Get my bow! My bow!

697
01:03:47.741 --> 01:03:48.867
Let's go!

698
01:03:58.293 --> 01:04:00.212
Let's go! Come on!

699
01:04:00.295 --> 01:04:01.713
And... pull!

700
01:04:01.797 --> 01:04:03.257
Wait!

701
01:04:03.841 --> 01:04:05.425
And... pull!

702
01:04:05.509 --> 01:04:06.844
Wait!

703
01:04:06.927 --> 01:04:08.387
And... pull!

704
01:04:08.971 --> 01:04:11.181
And... pull!

705
01:04:11.974 --> 01:04:13.976
And... pull!

706
01:04:14.059 --> 01:04:15.102
Go!

707
01:04:15.185 --> 01:04:17.479
- Wait! - And... pull!

708
01:04:18.730 --> 01:04:19.565
Wait!

709
01:04:30.576 --> 01:04:31.994
Push the oars!

710
01:04:37.833 --> 01:04:41.003
All right! Move this bastard!

711
01:05:34.723 --> 01:05:35.807
Turn to port.

712
01:05:35.891 --> 01:05:37.476
Follow the coast.

713
01:05:37.559 --> 01:05:39.061
What if they have ships?

714
01:05:39.144 --> 01:05:41.021
We need to get as far away as possible.

715
01:05:45.984 --> 01:05:48.236
We don't have supplies for a blind crossing.

716
01:05:48.946 --> 01:05:50.739
We've lost our way.

717
01:05:50.822 --> 01:05:53.909
We need to find food and get our bearings.

718
01:05:56.536 --> 01:05:57.537
Turn to port.

719
01:06:24.272 --> 01:06:27.275
There! Smoke! There!

720
01:06:27.359 --> 01:06:30.278
Go past, we'll find anchorage at the beach!

721
01:06:37.327 --> 01:06:38.787
Oars up!

722
01:06:42.499 --> 01:06:44.626
Form a landing party.

723
01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:48.005
Tomorrow.

724
01:06:50.298 --> 01:06:51.299
Tomorrow.

725
01:07:01.893 --> 01:07:03.603
Leave someone with the tenders.

726
01:07:04.563 --> 01:07:06.023
We think you should stay.

727
01:07:08.650 --> 01:07:10.318
- "We"? - All of us.

728
01:07:10.986 --> 01:07:12.195
Still alive.

729
01:07:22.998 --> 01:07:24.374
Is this a mutiny?

730
01:07:24.458 --> 01:07:25.834
That's up to you.

731
01:07:29.963 --> 01:07:30.964
Give me your bow.

732
01:07:31.048 --> 01:07:32.883
I'll make sure we eat tonight.

733
01:07:33.467 --> 01:07:34.843
No matter what you find.

734
01:09:21.449 --> 01:09:23.869
No, no! It's okay! It's okay!

735
01:09:27.831 --> 01:09:28.957
- Please don't hurt me! - It's okay.

736
01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:30.375
- You're okay! It's all right! - No!

737
01:09:30.458 --> 01:09:31.459
No one's gonna hurt you!

738
01:09:31.543 --> 01:09:33.253
If I do what you want.

739
01:09:33.336 --> 01:09:35.589
No one is going to hurt you.

740
01:09:39.384 --> 01:09:41.303
My name's Eurylochus. What's your name?

741
01:09:41.386 --> 01:09:42.470
Circe.

742
01:09:42.554 --> 01:09:45.432
Circe, we need food. Badly.

743
01:09:46.558 --> 01:09:47.851
I have food.

744
01:09:49.519 --> 01:09:50.937
Lots of food.

745
01:10:12.334 --> 01:10:13.418
Here, let me.

746
01:10:16.546 --> 01:10:18.465
Your weapons, your armor,

747
01:10:19.925 --> 01:10:21.009
put it out the back.

748
01:11:04.177 --> 01:11:05.804
You like my meat?

749
01:11:06.388 --> 01:11:08.890
Why... do you have so much?

750
01:11:09.724 --> 01:11:12.227
You never know when strangers will come.

751
01:11:22.696 --> 01:11:23.697
Men.

752
01:11:26.157 --> 01:11:27.867
With their appetites.

753
01:11:29.452 --> 01:11:31.329
Too much is never enough.

754
01:12:47.238 --> 01:12:48.823
Eurylochus!

755
01:13:14.432 --> 01:13:15.725
They haven't come back yet.

756
01:13:15.809 --> 01:13:16.935
- None of them? - No.

757
01:13:17.018 --> 01:13:18.186
Give me your dagger.

758
01:13:21.898 --> 01:13:22.982
Stay with the boat!

759
01:14:04.399 --> 01:14:05.859
Why don't you just come here?

760
01:14:14.242 --> 01:14:15.994
Do you come from the sea?

761
01:14:17.829 --> 01:14:19.164
Are you alone?

762
01:14:24.252 --> 01:14:26.463
My pigs have taken a liking to you.

763
01:14:27.255 --> 01:14:28.631
Are you hungry?

764
01:14:30.758 --> 01:14:32.594
Leave your weapons at the door.

765
01:14:32.677 --> 01:14:35.180
Enjoy the hospitality of Zeus.

766
01:14:36.556 --> 01:14:39.184
Grab a hog. You can slaughter it for your dinner.

767
01:15:04.584 --> 01:15:07.212
Whoever you are, you're clearly not a farmer.

768
01:15:07.295 --> 01:15:08.296
Here.

769
01:15:12.133 --> 01:15:13.593
That stew smells good.

770
01:15:17.222 --> 01:15:18.973
That's enough for just us.

771
01:15:20.016 --> 01:15:21.601
Who says it's just us?

772
01:15:23.102 --> 01:15:24.479
Who else is here?

773
01:15:25.063 --> 01:15:27.190
Who indeed?

774
01:15:39.285 --> 01:15:40.745
You say you're all alone.

775
01:15:41.579 --> 01:15:44.082
But no one comes here alone.

776
01:15:44.165 --> 01:15:45.542
No?

777
01:15:45.625 --> 01:15:48.086
You can only reach this spot by sea.

778
01:15:48.670 --> 01:15:51.089
So where are your shipmates?

779
01:15:51.965 --> 01:15:53.383
I'm looking for them.

780
01:15:54.300 --> 01:15:55.593
Anyone happen to come this way?

781
01:15:59.097 --> 01:16:00.682
But you prepared all this food?

782
01:16:00.765 --> 01:16:03.142
You're questioning me? You?

783
01:16:05.270 --> 01:16:07.438
A traveler who's clearly starving,

784
01:16:07.522 --> 01:16:09.774
yet hasn't touched the food in front of him.

785
01:16:09.857 --> 01:16:13.403
Claims to be a sailor, yet wears a great warrior's armor.

786
01:16:14.404 --> 01:16:16.197
And who breaks Zeus' Law

787
01:16:16.781 --> 01:16:19.701
by bringing a dagger into his hostess' house.

788
01:16:19.784 --> 01:16:21.077
You have sharp eyes.

789
01:16:21.160 --> 01:16:23.663
Sharpened by the gods.

790
01:16:23.746 --> 01:16:25.665
Do they grant you insights?

791
01:16:25.748 --> 01:16:27.125
Truths.

792
01:16:28.293 --> 01:16:29.711
Sometimes.

793
01:16:29.794 --> 01:16:31.838
Would they tell you where I could find my men?

794
01:16:32.797 --> 01:16:33.923
Your men?

795
01:16:34.007 --> 01:16:35.133
That's right.

796
01:16:35.758 --> 01:16:37.093
They would.

797
01:16:37.176 --> 01:16:38.303
And would you tell me?

798
01:16:38.928 --> 01:16:40.221
Perhaps.

799
01:16:41.931 --> 01:16:43.433
If you give me your dagger.

800
01:16:52.150 --> 01:16:55.778
But why would I help a liar who refuses my hospitality?

801
01:17:06.289 --> 01:17:09.125
Your men were here earlier, and now they're not.

802
01:17:15.798 --> 01:17:16.799
They moved on?

803
01:17:17.425 --> 01:17:18.885
Yes.

804
01:17:21.304 --> 01:17:22.930
Leaving their rings?

805
01:17:30.605 --> 01:17:31.606
I wouldn't.

806
01:17:33.399 --> 01:17:34.275
Don't.

807
01:17:34.359 --> 01:17:36.110
I don't know who this crow is to you,

808
01:17:36.194 --> 01:17:39.238
but if you don't put that knife down, I will throttle it to find out.

809
01:17:39.322 --> 01:17:40.615
Don't hurt her.

810
01:17:40.698 --> 01:17:41.908
Family?

811
01:17:43.785 --> 01:17:45.119
Mother?

812
01:17:47.330 --> 01:17:48.331
Your sister.

813
01:17:52.001 --> 01:17:54.712
You did this to her like you turned my men into pigs.

814
01:17:54.796 --> 01:17:56.339
I didn't turn your men into anything.

815
01:17:57.298 --> 01:18:00.009
Look at them. This is who they are. Look at them!

816
01:18:00.593 --> 01:18:02.970
Base. Disgusting.

817
01:18:03.054 --> 01:18:05.598
Primal urges that you value in battle.

818
01:18:05.682 --> 01:18:08.267
They've raped and pillaged across the world for you.

819
01:18:08.351 --> 01:18:10.144
And you think that's against their nature?

820
01:18:10.228 --> 01:18:12.480
You think they only did that because you ordered them to?

821
01:18:12.563 --> 01:18:16.567
A true commander thinks his men's savagery is actually discipline.

822
01:18:17.318 --> 01:18:19.404
But you don't seem that deluded.

823
01:18:19.487 --> 01:18:21.280
But you sent them to me here.

824
01:18:21.364 --> 01:18:22.907
On my own.

825
01:18:22.990 --> 01:18:26.869
A gang of filthy soldiers with empty bellies and hot blood!

826
01:18:28.162 --> 01:18:31.791
You thought they'd respect me in my home?

827
01:18:32.542 --> 01:18:33.793
Look at them!

828
01:18:33.876 --> 01:18:34.919
Know your own men.

829
01:18:36.713 --> 01:18:37.964
Did they hurt you?

830
01:18:38.047 --> 01:18:39.382
They would've.

831
01:18:39.966 --> 01:18:41.718
They just wanna go home.

832
01:18:45.096 --> 01:18:46.222
Let them.

833
01:19:12.415 --> 01:19:13.833
Come back.

834
01:19:14.876 --> 01:19:16.335
Come back.

835
01:19:17.170 --> 01:19:18.588
Come back.

836
01:19:18.671 --> 01:19:20.673
Back into your disguises.

837
01:19:24.886 --> 01:19:26.345
Come back.

838
01:19:26.429 --> 01:19:28.806
Back into your disguises.

839
01:19:28.890 --> 01:19:30.600
Come back.

840
01:19:30.683 --> 01:19:32.435
Back into your disguises.

841
01:19:32.977 --> 01:19:35.480
Come back. Come back.

842
01:19:36.564 --> 01:19:37.774
Come back.

843
01:19:37.857 --> 01:19:40.401
Back into your disguises.

844
01:19:40.485 --> 01:19:42.111
Come back.

845
01:19:45.782 --> 01:19:47.241
Odysseus.

846
01:19:47.325 --> 01:19:48.785
Back to the ship!

847
01:19:48.868 --> 01:19:50.578
Take all the food you can carry.

848
01:19:50.661 --> 01:19:52.288
Leave the meat.

849
01:19:52.371 --> 01:19:54.832
You're not gonna let her live. She's a witch.

850
01:19:54.916 --> 01:19:56.417
Who gave her powers?

851
01:19:56.501 --> 01:19:58.085
Your gods.

852
01:19:58.169 --> 01:20:00.129
Fill the jugs and skins at the stream.

853
01:20:00.213 --> 01:20:01.881
Be ready to sail at dawn.

854
01:20:07.428 --> 01:20:09.096
Why leave her like this?

855
01:20:09.680 --> 01:20:11.307
We get on better this way.

856
01:20:12.767 --> 01:20:14.602
I'm sorry if we offended you.

857
01:20:15.686 --> 01:20:17.897
We can't get back without your help.

858
01:20:17.980 --> 01:20:20.107
We need to know the way to the trade routes.

859
01:20:20.191 --> 01:20:21.901
You're too lost.

860
01:20:21.984 --> 01:20:23.027
What do you mean?

861
01:20:24.612 --> 01:20:27.365
Only blind Tiresias can tell you how to go home.

862
01:20:27.448 --> 01:20:28.449
Tiresias?

863
01:20:28.533 --> 01:20:30.117
In Hades.

864
01:20:30.201 --> 01:20:33.204
Nobody sails into Hell. And comes back.

865
01:20:33.287 --> 01:20:34.372
You will.

866
01:20:35.915 --> 01:20:37.708
Head north. Into the twilight.

867
01:20:43.506 --> 01:20:45.424
Then head inland.

868
01:20:45.508 --> 01:20:48.553
Until the river of fire meets a river of ice.

869
01:21:39.020 --> 01:21:40.563
There you must dig a trench.

870
01:21:43.733 --> 01:21:45.693
And fill it with the blood of sacrifice.

871
01:22:06.130 --> 01:22:07.506
Gather the dead.

872
01:22:08.758 --> 01:22:10.384
And learn how you go home.

873
01:22:12.470 --> 01:22:13.763
You.

874
01:22:14.805 --> 01:22:16.265
Not your men.

875
01:22:16.891 --> 01:22:18.517
You cursed them?

876
01:22:18.601 --> 01:22:19.810
Not a curse.

877
01:22:21.020 --> 01:22:22.229
A truth.

878
01:22:23.105 --> 01:22:25.900
Your men don't want you to defy the gods.

879
01:22:27.485 --> 01:22:29.737
But the gods say only you make it home.

880
01:22:30.821 --> 01:22:32.615
Then I defy the gods.

881
01:23:14.073 --> 01:23:15.116
Sinon.

882
01:23:15.783 --> 01:23:16.951
It's Odysseus.

883
01:23:21.664 --> 01:23:22.665
Sinon.

884
01:23:23.833 --> 01:23:24.834
It's Odysseus.

885
01:23:27.628 --> 01:23:29.338
Let him drink, Odysseus.

886
01:23:30.172 --> 01:23:32.008
Then you'll hear his testament.

887
01:23:46.480 --> 01:23:47.648
Odysseus?

888
01:23:47.732 --> 01:23:48.899
Yes.

889
01:23:48.983 --> 01:23:51.027
You lied to everyone.

890
01:23:54.905 --> 01:23:56.782
Did you have to lie to me?

891
01:23:57.742 --> 01:23:59.410
I needed you to believe.

892
01:24:01.370 --> 01:24:03.372
Why did you have to volunteer?

893
01:24:03.456 --> 01:24:05.249
Don't you remember how I came here?

894
01:24:05.332 --> 01:24:08.252
I begged to take Antinous' place.

895
01:24:08.335 --> 01:24:09.920
He'd been called.

896
01:24:10.629 --> 01:24:13.966
He offered money for your family if you'd take his place.

897
01:24:14.550 --> 01:24:16.594
If the Trojans don't let me live,

898
01:24:18.971 --> 01:24:20.431
if I never make it home,

899
01:24:20.514 --> 01:24:22.767
take this back to my father.

900
01:24:23.851 --> 01:24:25.478
Tell him everything I did.

901
01:24:28.522 --> 01:24:30.024
Odysseus.

902
01:24:31.358 --> 01:24:33.861
Feel free to add some stuff.

903
01:24:37.031 --> 01:24:39.450
You didn't trust me with the truth?

904
01:24:41.160 --> 01:24:43.287
You knew I'd die for you.

905
01:24:43.370 --> 01:24:46.248
Dying men tell the truth. I needed you to believe.

906
01:24:46.332 --> 01:24:49.126
I died for your lie on that beach.

907
01:24:49.877 --> 01:24:51.962
I know. I was there with you.

908
01:24:52.671 --> 01:24:55.591
I heard you repeat the lie with your dying breath.

909
01:24:55.674 --> 01:24:57.009
Winning us the war.

910
01:24:57.093 --> 01:24:58.803
I was right there. Inches from you.

911
01:24:58.886 --> 01:24:59.929
Smelling your blood.

912
01:25:00.012 --> 01:25:02.014
What comfort could that give me?

913
01:25:03.432 --> 01:25:07.394
Not knowing you were inside that horse until the dead told me.

914
01:25:08.395 --> 01:25:09.605
Which dead?

915
01:25:09.688 --> 01:25:13.317
A lot of veterans of your campaigns walk these shores.

916
01:25:15.528 --> 01:25:20.783
The dead meet to lament the living and greet the newly dead.

917
01:25:20.866 --> 01:25:23.285
My father was the first shade to find me.

918
01:25:23.994 --> 01:25:25.454
To comfort you.

919
01:25:25.538 --> 01:25:26.997
Shades don't comfort.

920
01:25:27.706 --> 01:25:30.876
They want word of the living who wronged them.

921
01:25:31.752 --> 01:25:34.255
My father had died in poverty,

922
01:25:34.338 --> 01:25:39.301
alone, begging for scraps from people like Antinous.

923
01:25:39.385 --> 01:25:41.178
Antinous cheated you?

924
01:25:41.804 --> 01:25:43.931
As he's no doubt cheating you.

925
01:25:44.014 --> 01:25:44.849
How?

926
01:25:44.932 --> 01:25:47.393
Who's in Ithaca looking after your wife and son?

927
01:25:47.476 --> 01:25:49.937
My wife is of no concern.

928
01:25:51.147 --> 01:25:53.649
Talk to Agamemnon about homecomings.

929
01:25:54.275 --> 01:25:55.693
Wait. Agamemnon's here?

930
01:25:59.071 --> 01:26:01.365
You buried me at Troy with respect.

931
01:26:01.448 --> 01:26:02.575
Thank you.

932
01:26:03.284 --> 01:26:05.119
But spare a thought for those you didn't.

933
01:26:05.703 --> 01:26:07.663
It's not always possible.

934
01:26:08.247 --> 01:26:11.375
When you get home, take a ship.

935
01:26:11.458 --> 01:26:14.670
Head into the unknown west till you find land.

936
01:26:14.753 --> 01:26:16.755
And there, give sacrifice

937
01:26:16.839 --> 01:26:18.966
and honor your men.

938
01:26:20.551 --> 01:26:22.678
Do you still have the lot?

939
01:26:25.848 --> 01:26:27.516
Of course.

940
01:26:27.600 --> 01:26:28.601
If you make it home,

941
01:26:28.684 --> 01:26:30.769
give Antinous back his shame.

942
01:26:30.853 --> 01:26:31.979
I will.

943
01:26:43.365 --> 01:26:44.408
My lord.

944
01:26:56.003 --> 01:26:57.296
Odysseus.

945
01:26:58.088 --> 01:26:59.298
How are you here?

946
01:26:59.840 --> 01:27:01.884
We won the war. You headed for home.

947
01:27:02.384 --> 01:27:05.971
Odysseus, the most brilliant of all my generals.

948
01:27:07.264 --> 01:27:08.933
You would never be snared as I was.

949
01:27:09.016 --> 01:27:10.893
Were you cast adrift? Like us?

950
01:27:11.393 --> 01:27:12.394
No.

951
01:27:12.478 --> 01:27:14.480
My misfortune was to make it home.

952
01:27:15.648 --> 01:27:17.983
To be welcomed by my beautiful wife.

953
01:27:23.989 --> 01:27:25.115
Bathed.

954
01:27:27.618 --> 01:27:28.953
Anointed.

955
01:27:31.080 --> 01:27:32.373
Made soft.

956
01:27:33.165 --> 01:27:34.625
To learn the hardest lesson.

957
01:27:35.709 --> 01:27:38.545
That I'd left a poison in her heart years before.

958
01:27:41.215 --> 01:27:42.591
Good part of her heart had gone to another.

959
01:27:45.886 --> 01:27:48.347
No! No.

960
01:27:49.223 --> 01:27:51.850
She and her lover had plotted my murder for years.

961
01:27:53.310 --> 01:27:54.353
Odysseus.

962
01:27:55.479 --> 01:27:56.689
Yes.

963
01:27:56.772 --> 01:28:00.276
My son Orestes, did he avenge me?

964
01:28:00.359 --> 01:28:01.652
My lord, how could I know?

965
01:28:02.778 --> 01:28:04.405
I'm still trying to get home.

966
01:28:05.531 --> 01:28:07.032
Don't make my mistake.

967
01:28:08.158 --> 01:28:11.287
Don't walk in the front door expecting garlands and praise.

968
01:28:11.996 --> 01:28:13.247
Come in disguise.

969
01:28:14.164 --> 01:28:15.207
Take your time.

970
01:28:16.083 --> 01:28:17.209
Assess.

971
01:28:24.008 --> 01:28:25.467
Do you see?

972
01:28:27.303 --> 01:28:28.762
I know what I have to do.

973
01:28:28.846 --> 01:28:30.556
Some of what you have to do.

974
01:28:30.639 --> 01:28:33.225
My prophecy is not for their ears.

975
01:28:34.435 --> 01:28:35.602
Back to the ship.

976
01:28:37.688 --> 01:28:38.772
Sail southwest.

977
01:28:38.856 --> 01:28:40.607
Past the sirens.

978
01:28:41.692 --> 01:28:43.944
Their song lures your men onto the rocks.

979
01:28:44.028 --> 01:28:45.029
They all die.

980
01:28:45.112 --> 01:28:46.572
I won't let that happen.

981
01:28:47.114 --> 01:28:49.575
I'll block their ears. With wax.

982
01:28:49.658 --> 01:28:50.701
Past the sirens

983
01:28:50.784 --> 01:28:53.329
you choose between the whirlpool Charybdis

984
01:28:53.412 --> 01:28:54.705
and the monster Scylla.

985
01:28:54.788 --> 01:28:57.499
The whirlpool in the sea takes all of your lives.

986
01:28:57.583 --> 01:28:59.877
The monster in the cliffs, only six.

987
01:28:59.960 --> 01:29:01.628
But your determination

988
01:29:01.712 --> 01:29:04.173
to not sacrifice any more lives kills all of them.

989
01:29:05.382 --> 01:29:06.425
I'll do what I have to.

990
01:29:06.508 --> 01:29:09.345
At the end, when starvation and mutiny threaten,

991
01:29:09.428 --> 01:29:13.098
they demand to land where the Sun God grazes his cattle.

992
01:29:13.724 --> 01:29:15.434
They slaughter Apollo's herd.

993
01:29:16.685 --> 01:29:18.312
Zeus kills them for it.

994
01:29:18.395 --> 01:29:19.646
Do you see?

995
01:29:20.773 --> 01:29:22.107
I'll stop them.

996
01:29:22.191 --> 01:29:25.486
Why do you value these men's lives more than those you've already lost?

997
01:29:25.569 --> 01:29:27.613
I can still save them from the gods.

998
01:29:27.696 --> 01:29:30.115
But not from themselves.

999
01:29:33.118 --> 01:29:34.161
What is that?

1000
01:29:34.244 --> 01:29:35.829
Your unburied crew.

1001
01:29:39.375 --> 01:29:41.585
Demanding you share their dishonor.

1002
01:29:43.587 --> 01:29:44.797
Let's go!

1003
01:30:00.437 --> 01:30:01.730
Cast off!

1004
01:30:02.523 --> 01:30:04.775
Cast off! Move it!

1005
01:30:29.716 --> 01:30:30.759
Is that our fate?

1006
01:30:33.637 --> 01:30:35.305
Is that our fate?

1007
01:30:55.951 --> 01:30:58.078
We rowed out of Hell,

1008
01:30:59.163 --> 01:31:01.623
leaving so many fallen comrades.

1009
01:31:02.416 --> 01:31:04.251
I have to save the rest.

1010
01:31:04.960 --> 01:31:06.003
What's happened to them?

1011
01:31:07.004 --> 01:31:08.297
Did they leave me here?

1012
01:31:08.380 --> 01:31:10.007
Are they coming back?

1013
01:31:11.925 --> 01:31:13.552
Did you do something to them?

1014
01:31:15.137 --> 01:31:16.847
Did you do something to them?

1015
01:31:16.930 --> 01:31:18.098
Have some.

1016
01:31:18.724 --> 01:31:20.934
You told me not to eat the lotus flower.

1017
01:31:21.018 --> 01:31:22.060
Just tonight.

1018
01:31:22.895 --> 01:31:24.563
So you can get some sleep.

1019
01:31:27.858 --> 01:31:31.445
And tomorrow, when you're ready, you'll remember.

1020
01:31:34.156 --> 01:31:36.283
You'll remember your crew.

1021
01:31:36.366 --> 01:31:37.826
And your family.

1022
01:31:37.910 --> 01:31:39.119
Family.

1023
01:31:40.871 --> 01:31:41.997
A wife?

1024
01:31:43.832 --> 01:31:45.250
Do I have a wife?

1025
01:31:49.838 --> 01:31:52.716
- What are you doing? - It's all right, Melantho.

1026
01:31:52.799 --> 01:31:54.426
Strangers don't come in here.

1027
01:31:54.510 --> 01:31:55.719
Screen or no screen,

1028
01:31:55.802 --> 01:31:59.181
after three years of feasting together, we're far from strangers, Penelope.

1029
01:31:59.264 --> 01:32:02.518
Are you trying to marry me or ruin my reputation?

1030
01:32:02.601 --> 01:32:03.644
Melantho can be discreet.

1031
01:32:04.353 --> 01:32:07.773
I'm actually here out of concern for your reputation.

1032
01:32:08.941 --> 01:32:10.567
Everyone sees how hard you work.

1033
01:32:10.651 --> 01:32:13.195
Slow, steady progress.

1034
01:32:14.571 --> 01:32:16.949
But, every night, you've been unraveling that work.

1035
01:32:22.746 --> 01:32:23.789
Leave us.

1036
01:32:23.872 --> 01:32:26.708
You can't expect her

1037
01:32:26.792 --> 01:32:29.253
to be loyal to a master she never even knew.

1038
01:32:29.336 --> 01:32:31.213
I expect her to be loyal to me.

1039
01:32:31.296 --> 01:32:32.256
And she will be.

1040
01:32:32.339 --> 01:32:33.966
When you're married to me.

1041
01:32:35.008 --> 01:32:38.554
Slaves have no choice but to be loyal to the future

1042
01:32:38.637 --> 01:32:40.847
and this is a household waiting for a master,

1043
01:32:40.931 --> 01:32:43.517
just as Ithaca's a kingdom waiting for a king.

1044
01:32:45.936 --> 01:32:47.729
Ithaca's king is coming back.

1045
01:32:47.813 --> 01:32:49.147
No, he's not.

1046
01:32:51.024 --> 01:32:53.777
Do you know that, as a boy, I tried to go with him to Troy?

1047
01:32:55.028 --> 01:32:56.280
He never told me that.

1048
01:32:57.823 --> 01:32:59.074
Odysseus refused to let me.

1049
01:32:59.157 --> 01:33:02.619
He knew that Ithaca needed a future without him.

1050
01:33:09.376 --> 01:33:11.461
And he saw me as part of that future.

1051
01:33:16.216 --> 01:33:21.346
I'll never tell other suitors about your... Deception.

1052
01:33:23.682 --> 01:33:25.183
Finish your weaving.

1053
01:33:27.227 --> 01:33:28.645
It's time to let him go.

1054
01:33:37.946 --> 01:33:40.532
Why would you want to marry someone who loves someone else?

1055
01:33:43.535 --> 01:33:48.206
Your loyalty to Odysseus is one of your finest qualities.

1056
01:33:51.543 --> 01:33:52.878
I love you.

1057
01:33:53.503 --> 01:33:55.297
I want you to choose me.

1058
01:33:55.380 --> 01:33:57.716
And end all this.

1059
01:33:58.258 --> 01:34:00.844
You can't tell me you're not tired.

1060
01:34:01.553 --> 01:34:04.139
The waiting. The uncertainty.

1061
01:34:06.266 --> 01:34:08.852
You've done your duty.

1062
01:34:10.020 --> 01:34:13.649
Year after year after year.

1063
01:34:16.526 --> 01:34:18.111
It's time to think of yourself.

1064
01:34:19.946 --> 01:34:21.365
It's time to live again.

1065
01:34:26.870 --> 01:34:28.538
It's what Odysseus would've wanted.

1066
01:34:35.462 --> 01:34:36.838
I'll take care of all your needs.

1067
01:34:41.635 --> 01:34:42.761
My Queen.

1068
01:34:45.889 --> 01:34:47.432
And my son?

1069
01:34:48.558 --> 01:34:50.018
The throne is his.

1070
01:34:50.102 --> 01:34:51.103
He's the heir.

1071
01:34:53.605 --> 01:34:56.024
Unless we have a child of our own.

1072
01:35:06.201 --> 01:35:07.703
Where is Telemachus?

1073
01:35:09.287 --> 01:35:10.747
He's gone inland.

1074
01:35:10.831 --> 01:35:13.083
To visit his grandfather.

1075
01:35:20.465 --> 01:35:21.925
She's lying.

1076
01:35:22.008 --> 01:35:23.468
He's gone to sea.

1077
01:35:37.941 --> 01:35:39.651
He's gone for news of his father.

1078
01:35:40.277 --> 01:35:41.278
Where?

1079
01:35:41.361 --> 01:35:43.947
He's gone to Sparta. To Menelaus.

1080
01:35:45.282 --> 01:35:46.742
Well, should we go there?

1081
01:35:47.534 --> 01:35:48.702
No.

1082
01:35:49.870 --> 01:35:53.206
Too loud, Melanthius. I told you you're a cowherd.

1083
01:35:54.332 --> 01:35:55.667
- He was listening. - No, no.

1084
01:35:55.751 --> 01:35:57.043
No. No.

1085
01:35:59.421 --> 01:36:00.464
I was coming for Argus.

1086
01:36:02.090 --> 01:36:03.508
I told you that dog stays there.

1087
01:36:03.592 --> 01:36:04.968
Go back to the farm.

1088
01:36:05.051 --> 01:36:06.720
When we need your pigs, we'll send Melanthius.

1089
01:36:07.721 --> 01:36:09.931
You're no longer welcome in this palace, old man.

1090
01:36:10.015 --> 01:36:11.975
I answer to my mistress.

1091
01:36:14.311 --> 01:36:15.812
She even know your name?

1092
01:36:17.147 --> 01:36:18.190
Now go.

1093
01:36:18.774 --> 01:36:22.027
Telemachus will have to come back by the temple at Pylos.

1094
01:36:22.903 --> 01:36:24.404
Take your men and wait for him there.

1095
01:36:24.488 --> 01:36:26.740
Why, so you can stay and try and charm the Queen?

1096
01:36:26.823 --> 01:36:28.158
I don't think so.

1097
01:36:29.075 --> 01:36:30.744
He needs to die on that road.

1098
01:36:30.827 --> 01:36:32.454
And Irus has a plan.

1099
01:36:33.246 --> 01:36:34.414
Irus!

1100
01:36:36.750 --> 01:36:38.460
I need everyone in his party dead.

1101
01:36:38.543 --> 01:36:40.545
In a ditch. Throats open.

1102
01:36:40.629 --> 01:36:43.048
Telemachus and his men will be ready for the bandits

1103
01:36:43.131 --> 01:36:45.383
who work the road between here and Pylos.

1104
01:36:46.426 --> 01:36:48.720
But if some of them took the temple from the priests

1105
01:36:48.804 --> 01:36:49.846
before they arrived...

1106
01:36:51.264 --> 01:36:53.308
And bring me part of Telemachus as proof.

1107
01:36:54.810 --> 01:36:55.894
His nose.

1108
01:36:56.520 --> 01:36:58.313
Would you know him only by his nose?

1109
01:37:01.900 --> 01:37:03.151
His ears too.

1110
01:37:08.365 --> 01:37:09.991
Who taught you?

1111
01:37:10.075 --> 01:37:11.618
My father's swineherd.

1112
01:37:11.701 --> 01:37:12.786
The swineherder.

1113
01:37:12.869 --> 01:37:14.496
Yeah, his most loyal servant.

1114
01:37:15.080 --> 01:37:18.083
Odysseus left his most loyal servant behind.

1115
01:37:18.166 --> 01:37:19.626
He always was the smartest.

1116
01:37:20.627 --> 01:37:23.004
Only one Agamemnon ever listened to.

1117
01:37:23.839 --> 01:37:25.340
Should have listened harder.

1118
01:37:25.423 --> 01:37:27.259
Can I ask what happened to your brother?

1119
01:37:27.342 --> 01:37:28.593
His wife betrayed him.

1120
01:37:28.677 --> 01:37:29.845
Murdered him.

1121
01:37:30.512 --> 01:37:31.555
What did you do to them?

1122
01:37:31.638 --> 01:37:33.807
His son Orestes, he killed them both.

1123
01:37:34.683 --> 01:37:36.017
What, he killed his own mother?

1124
01:37:37.310 --> 01:37:38.353
He avenged his father.

1125
01:37:39.437 --> 01:37:40.814
But you don't have to worry.

1126
01:37:40.897 --> 01:37:43.608
Your mother has nothing in common with Clytemnestra.

1127
01:37:43.692 --> 01:37:44.693
Maybe not.

1128
01:37:44.776 --> 01:37:46.611
But the suitors have convinced the elders

1129
01:37:46.695 --> 01:37:48.822
it's time she remarried.

1130
01:37:48.905 --> 01:37:50.907
It's not a good time for an empty throne.

1131
01:37:51.408 --> 01:37:53.535
You've heard the stories. People from the sea.

1132
01:37:53.618 --> 01:37:54.786
Have you been raided?

1133
01:37:54.870 --> 01:37:57.289
Not yet, but the attacks, they do seem closer.

1134
01:37:57.372 --> 01:37:58.748
Sparta's ready.

1135
01:37:58.832 --> 01:38:00.584
What about Ithaca?

1136
01:38:00.667 --> 01:38:02.919
Would she be able to raise an army, defend herself?

1137
01:38:03.003 --> 01:38:04.379
Someone's gotta take the throne.

1138
01:38:04.462 --> 01:38:06.923
Well, then the battle is there, not here, son.

1139
01:38:07.716 --> 01:38:09.301
You've run from the problem.

1140
01:38:10.218 --> 01:38:12.721
And left your mother at the mercy of the suitors.

1141
01:38:14.055 --> 01:38:15.891
Should I hire mercenaries?

1142
01:38:15.974 --> 01:38:18.226
Go back and kill them all?

1143
01:38:18.310 --> 01:38:20.437
Murder rich and powerful guests in your own home?

1144
01:38:21.104 --> 01:38:22.564
You'd be exiled.

1145
01:38:22.647 --> 01:38:24.232
What then?

1146
01:38:24.316 --> 01:38:27.777
You must evict them without violating Zeus' Law.

1147
01:38:29.321 --> 01:38:30.906
I have to take the throne.

1148
01:38:32.198 --> 01:38:34.117
I have to know if Odysseus is alive.

1149
01:38:34.659 --> 01:38:37.329
This is nothing to do with a father you've never known.

1150
01:38:37.954 --> 01:38:39.706
You have to lead, son.

1151
01:38:39.789 --> 01:38:41.333
No matter what's happened to Odysseus.

1152
01:38:55.597 --> 01:38:56.973
You need to remember.

1153
01:38:57.057 --> 01:38:58.350
No, I don't.

1154
01:38:59.267 --> 01:39:01.978
I don't need to know the truth. I'm happy here. With you.

1155
01:39:04.272 --> 01:39:08.068
Then why are you gathering the bones of your old ship to make a raft?

1156
01:39:10.779 --> 01:39:12.280
Remember your crew.

1157
01:39:13.698 --> 01:39:14.741
Your journey.

1158
01:39:18.787 --> 01:39:21.456
I heard the song of the sirens.

1159
01:39:30.840 --> 01:39:32.717
Put the wax in the men's ears.

1160
01:39:33.677 --> 01:39:36.429
- None for me. Tie me to the mast. - Why?

1161
01:39:36.513 --> 01:39:37.973
I wanna hear the sirens' song.

1162
01:39:38.056 --> 01:39:38.974
No.

1163
01:39:39.933 --> 01:39:41.226
That's a bad idea.

1164
01:39:41.309 --> 01:39:42.727
No one's ever heard the song and lived.

1165
01:39:42.811 --> 01:39:45.105
That's why it's a good idea. I'll be the first.

1166
01:39:46.189 --> 01:39:47.565
No matter how hard I plead,

1167
01:39:47.649 --> 01:39:50.068
do not untie me until we're well clear.

1168
01:39:51.319 --> 01:39:52.862
Check the men's ears.

1169
01:39:53.613 --> 01:39:56.408
A week's rations for the first, "Yes, sir"!

1170
01:40:54.883 --> 01:40:56.384
What was the sirens' song?

1171
01:40:57.677 --> 01:40:59.804
All the things you want it to be.

1172
01:41:00.555 --> 01:41:04.225
Then all the things you wish you never wished for.

1173
01:41:22.160 --> 01:41:25.371
It was the delicious itch you go to scratch,

1174
01:41:26.498 --> 01:41:29.000
but find it's under the skin. You can't reach it.

1175
01:41:31.294 --> 01:41:34.047
So the delicious itch becomes unbearable.

1176
01:41:38.343 --> 01:41:40.470
Told you what you most want

1177
01:41:42.472 --> 01:41:44.474
is what you most can't have.

1178
01:41:48.061 --> 01:41:49.896
And what you most can't have

1179
01:41:51.189 --> 01:41:53.358
is what you already had.

1180
01:41:53.441 --> 01:41:55.026
And lost.

1181
01:42:03.284 --> 01:42:05.829
It was the song of all the promises I failed to keep.

1182
01:42:11.167 --> 01:42:13.628
And it told me I don't really wanna go home.

1183
01:42:16.673 --> 01:42:18.591
White water!

1184
01:42:18.675 --> 01:42:19.717
White water ahead!

1185
01:42:22.637 --> 01:42:23.680
Charybdis.

1186
01:42:24.722 --> 01:42:26.391
A monstrous whirlpool.

1187
01:42:29.102 --> 01:42:30.103
Hoist the sails!

1188
01:42:30.770 --> 01:42:32.188
Come on, let's go!

1189
01:42:32.272 --> 01:42:34.149
Get the windlass up!

1190
01:42:34.232 --> 01:42:35.567
Can we head out to sea?

1191
01:42:35.650 --> 01:42:36.943
It's already got us.

1192
01:42:38.945 --> 01:42:40.363
Man those ropes!

1193
01:42:40.446 --> 01:42:42.240
We wanna head north! Move it!

1194
01:42:43.533 --> 01:42:45.577
- We might make that canyon. - It's too narrow.

1195
01:42:45.660 --> 01:42:47.453
Make for the whirlpool.

1196
01:42:47.537 --> 01:42:49.080
If you steer down the slope,

1197
01:42:49.164 --> 01:42:51.583
we can make enough way to shoot out the other side.

1198
01:42:51.666 --> 01:42:53.334
Don't be afraid. We can do it.

1199
01:42:58.339 --> 01:43:00.175
No. No!

1200
01:43:00.884 --> 01:43:02.302
We're making for the channel.

1201
01:43:02.385 --> 01:43:04.137
- Get a grip. - We're making for the channel!

1202
01:43:04.220 --> 01:43:05.263
Oars in!

1203
01:43:07.307 --> 01:43:08.349
Get the oars in!

1204
01:43:09.100 --> 01:43:11.769
Starboard oars, prepare to hold water!

1205
01:43:18.484 --> 01:43:19.694
On my signal!

1206
01:43:21.112 --> 01:43:22.113
Three...

1207
01:43:24.324 --> 01:43:25.450
Two...

1208
01:43:26.826 --> 01:43:28.328
One! Hard!

1209
01:43:29.662 --> 01:43:31.998
Oars out!

1210
01:43:42.091 --> 01:43:43.134
Hold it!

1211
01:44:16.709 --> 01:44:18.211
We made it!

1212
01:44:18.294 --> 01:44:19.295
We did!

1213
01:44:20.213 --> 01:44:22.090
We did it. The gods were with us...

1214
01:44:25.802 --> 01:44:26.761
Pull!

1215
01:44:28.179 --> 01:44:29.222
Pull!

1216
01:44:29.931 --> 01:44:31.474
Pull!

1217
01:44:31.557 --> 01:44:32.767
Pull!

1218
01:44:32.850 --> 01:44:34.519
Keep us straight!

1219
01:44:36.938 --> 01:44:38.356
Pull!

1220
01:44:40.483 --> 01:44:41.776
Pull!

1221
01:44:41.859 --> 01:44:43.152
Keep us straight!

1222
01:44:43.236 --> 01:44:46.698
Pull! Pull!

1223
01:44:49.075 --> 01:44:50.076
Pull!

1224
01:45:10.221 --> 01:45:11.222
You knew.

1225
01:45:11.764 --> 01:45:12.807
And you didn't tell us.

1226
01:45:13.683 --> 01:45:15.810
If I told you, you wouldn't have chosen this way.

1227
01:45:15.893 --> 01:45:17.895
And those men would still be alive!

1228
01:45:17.979 --> 01:45:18.980
No!

1229
01:45:19.063 --> 01:45:20.523
We'd all be dead.

1230
01:45:20.606 --> 01:45:22.150
You had no right!

1231
01:45:22.233 --> 01:45:23.568
I had every right.

1232
01:45:23.651 --> 01:45:25.278
And every responsibility!

1233
01:45:25.361 --> 01:45:28.406
The whirlpool would have destroyed the ship and all of us.

1234
01:45:28.489 --> 01:45:29.532
This way, we live.

1235
01:45:29.615 --> 01:45:30.700
Some of us live!

1236
01:45:30.783 --> 01:45:32.201
Yes!

1237
01:45:35.496 --> 01:45:36.956
What else did Tiresias tell you?

1238
01:45:49.052 --> 01:45:50.470
I'm gonna put in there.

1239
01:45:52.889 --> 01:45:56.184
We can't land there. That's the Sun God's isle.

1240
01:45:56.267 --> 01:45:57.643
We need repairs.

1241
01:45:58.853 --> 01:46:00.605
These men are exhausted!

1242
01:46:00.688 --> 01:46:01.939
We lost six shipmates!

1243
01:46:02.565 --> 01:46:05.151
We're gonna put in. We're gonna rest up on dry land.

1244
01:46:05.234 --> 01:46:07.236
You're all gonna die!

1245
01:46:09.822 --> 01:46:11.824
You asked me what Tiresias told me?

1246
01:46:12.742 --> 01:46:14.285
He said you're all gonna die.

1247
01:46:14.952 --> 01:46:20.083
If you slaughter the Sun God's cattle which graze on that island.

1248
01:46:20.166 --> 01:46:21.250
You'll all die.

1249
01:46:25.588 --> 01:46:26.631
That's fine.

1250
01:46:27.590 --> 01:46:28.716
We'll put in.

1251
01:46:29.425 --> 01:46:30.676
We'll cook our own provisions.

1252
01:46:30.760 --> 01:46:33.846
We'll pick fruit. We'll get water. Nothing more.

1253
01:46:35.139 --> 01:46:36.557
Swear to it.

1254
01:46:37.058 --> 01:46:38.226
All of you.

1255
01:46:52.031 --> 01:46:53.032
See?

1256
01:46:54.033 --> 01:46:55.576
The wind took us right in.

1257
01:46:56.702 --> 01:46:59.664
We'll rest up. Do the repairs.

1258
01:46:59.747 --> 01:47:01.999
We'll set out as soon as the wind changes.

1259
01:47:02.750 --> 01:47:03.751
Grab that line.

1260
01:47:14.929 --> 01:47:16.931
Did the crew keep their promise?

1261
01:47:17.974 --> 01:47:19.100
Yes.

1262
01:47:19.725 --> 01:47:21.102
For a long time.

1263
01:47:22.687 --> 01:47:24.230
But the wind never changed.

1264
01:47:24.313 --> 01:47:28.359
The steady, on-shore breeze that made it so easy for us to land

1265
01:47:30.862 --> 01:47:32.405
made it impossible to leave.

1266
01:47:36.409 --> 01:47:38.369
And our provisions ran out.

1267
01:47:41.581 --> 01:47:42.665
Go.

1268
01:48:45.269 --> 01:48:48.481
Did you think if you ate quietly the Sun God wouldn't notice?

1269
01:48:48.564 --> 01:48:50.274
We thought you wouldn't notice.

1270
01:48:50.900 --> 01:48:52.401
I'm not the problem.

1271
01:48:52.485 --> 01:48:54.111
I wanted you to live.

1272
01:48:59.784 --> 01:49:01.244
Tiresias told you.

1273
01:49:01.327 --> 01:49:03.579
Circe told you. The gods told you.

1274
01:49:04.247 --> 01:49:06.707
You gods don't speak in ways we understand.

1275
01:49:06.791 --> 01:49:08.459
Who doesn't understand pain?

1276
01:49:09.293 --> 01:49:10.711
Or blood or death?

1277
01:49:11.546 --> 01:49:14.632
When you watch your men die, do you see chance or do you see consequence?

1278
01:49:15.174 --> 01:49:17.843
I see consequence. I see my failure.

1279
01:49:18.344 --> 01:49:21.055
What if their deaths aren't your failure but theirs?

1280
01:49:21.138 --> 01:49:22.390
I lead them.

1281
01:49:23.808 --> 01:49:25.351
I led them here.

1282
01:49:25.434 --> 01:49:26.811
And who led you?

1283
01:49:27.937 --> 01:49:29.522
Why do you put yourself above the gods?

1284
01:49:29.605 --> 01:49:31.440
Why do you claim responsibility?

1285
01:49:32.400 --> 01:49:34.068
Why don't you wanna go home?

1286
01:49:34.151 --> 01:49:35.820
What do you expect?

1287
01:49:37.280 --> 01:49:38.781
They ran out of food.

1288
01:49:39.824 --> 01:49:40.825
Have some pity.

1289
01:49:40.908 --> 01:49:43.828
My pity is what put us on this damned island.

1290
01:49:43.911 --> 01:49:45.246
I told you what would happen.

1291
01:49:45.329 --> 01:49:46.455
Yes, you did.

1292
01:49:47.164 --> 01:49:50.251
And we decided we would rather drown than starve.

1293
01:49:55.756 --> 01:49:57.967
Well, now's our chance.

1294
01:49:58.050 --> 01:50:00.303
The wind has dropped! The wind has dropped!

1295
01:50:04.265 --> 01:50:08.060
The calm was just long enough to lure us out onto Poseidon's ocean.

1296
01:50:09.353 --> 01:50:11.188
Under Zeus' sky.

1297
01:50:11.272 --> 01:50:14.400
Get us into the wind!

1298
01:50:14.483 --> 01:50:16.444
Port! Port!

1299
01:50:30.291 --> 01:50:31.792
Pray to the gods!

1300
01:50:32.918 --> 01:50:35.838
- Get us into the wind! - Pray for forgiveness!

1301
01:50:35.921 --> 01:50:37.173
Wind!

1302
01:50:52.813 --> 01:50:54.649
Get us into the wind!

1303
01:50:55.441 --> 01:50:56.942
Into the wind!

1304
01:50:59.570 --> 01:51:01.113
Odysseus!

1305
01:51:02.740 --> 01:51:04.241
Get us into the wind!

1306
01:51:17.755 --> 01:51:19.632
Give yourself to the gods!

1307
01:51:23.636 --> 01:51:25.930
Pray to Poseidon!

1308
01:51:32.395 --> 01:51:34.939
Pray for forgiveness!

1309
01:51:46.784 --> 01:51:47.952
Look out!

1310
01:52:02.967 --> 01:52:05.886
The last thing I remember was the harsh hand of Zeus.

1311
01:52:18.315 --> 01:52:20.025
Eurylochus!

1312
01:52:50.848 --> 01:52:52.266
I don't know what happened to my men.

1313
01:52:52.349 --> 01:52:53.559
Yes, you do.

1314
01:52:55.644 --> 01:52:57.438
Exactly what you told them would happen.

1315
01:52:57.938 --> 01:52:59.899
I washed up on your shore alone.

1316
01:52:59.982 --> 01:53:02.359
You washed up on my shore

1317
01:53:02.443 --> 01:53:04.945
with some of your boat and none of your crew.

1318
01:53:06.947 --> 01:53:09.200
Half dead, unconscious.

1319
01:53:31.472 --> 01:53:33.974
I watched you lost for days...

1320
01:53:34.058 --> 01:53:35.851
No, no, no.

1321
01:53:35.935 --> 01:53:37.603
...into weeks.

1322
01:53:38.270 --> 01:53:40.147
I knew the lotus would ease your awakening.

1323
01:53:40.231 --> 01:53:42.525
No, no, no.

1324
01:53:42.608 --> 01:53:45.319
So that your pain was more of the body than the mind.

1325
01:53:48.072 --> 01:53:49.198
And it did.

1326
01:53:51.742 --> 01:53:53.494
You liked it so much.

1327
01:53:54.662 --> 01:53:56.622
You ate it so willingly.

1328
01:54:00.334 --> 01:54:01.752
And as I cared for you...

1329
01:54:02.378 --> 01:54:03.504
Thank you.

1330
01:54:03.587 --> 01:54:05.506
...I came to love you.

1331
01:54:10.553 --> 01:54:12.263
How long have I been here?

1332
01:54:14.348 --> 01:54:17.017
I kept telling myself to stop giving it to you,

1333
01:54:17.101 --> 01:54:19.061
that you were strong enough to remember.

1334
01:54:19.144 --> 01:54:21.272
- How long, Calypso? - I mean, you were so happy.

1335
01:54:21.355 --> 01:54:24.900
- You were happy. - We were so happy. For years.

1336
01:54:24.984 --> 01:54:26.068
Years?

1337
01:54:32.992 --> 01:54:33.993
Seven years.

1338
01:54:35.244 --> 01:54:38.831
You fed me lotus flower and held me captive.

1339
01:54:38.914 --> 01:54:40.124
I saved your life.

1340
01:54:41.292 --> 01:54:42.334
I rebuilt your body.

1341
01:54:42.418 --> 01:54:45.337
I just couldn't bring myself to rebuild your mind.

1342
01:54:46.505 --> 01:54:48.048
You have been happy.

1343
01:54:49.133 --> 01:54:52.344
Your heart was just always somewhere else.

1344
01:54:53.470 --> 01:54:57.099
And your mind was bound to follow eventually.

1345
01:54:57.933 --> 01:54:59.268
How could you?

1346
01:54:59.351 --> 01:55:02.521
The lotus clouds the mind and saps the will,

1347
01:55:02.605 --> 01:55:04.607
but it doesn't change who you are.

1348
01:55:05.190 --> 01:55:08.027
You weren't ready to go home, for whatever reason.

1349
01:55:08.110 --> 01:55:09.778
I am now.

1350
01:55:11.280 --> 01:55:13.032
As I always knew you would be.

1351
01:55:13.657 --> 01:55:14.658
How?

1352
01:55:14.742 --> 01:55:17.661
You were clutching something when I found you.

1353
01:55:17.745 --> 01:55:19.413
Your hand wouldn't open for weeks.

1354
01:55:20.372 --> 01:55:21.749
You'd call out.

1355
01:55:21.832 --> 01:55:23.500
Mostly to Athena,

1356
01:55:23.584 --> 01:55:26.503
but sometimes you would say another name.

1357
01:55:40.601 --> 01:55:42.019
Penelope.

1358
01:55:45.981 --> 01:55:47.483
Help me go home.

1359
01:55:48.108 --> 01:55:49.276
I am.

1360
01:55:50.527 --> 01:55:53.072
You're a man who needs to control his fate.

1361
01:55:54.573 --> 01:55:56.116
But you cannot control this.

1362
01:55:59.411 --> 01:56:01.622
You must give yourself to the storm.

1363
01:56:02.289 --> 01:56:03.916
Give yourself to Poseidon.

1364
01:56:05.376 --> 01:56:06.877
And take your punishment.

1365
01:56:09.672 --> 01:56:11.256
Zeus won't let him kill you.

1366
01:56:14.885 --> 01:56:18.347
Give up the fight, give up control, and live.

1367
01:56:21.183 --> 01:56:25.062
It's a leap of faith you've never yet made, Odysseus.

1368
01:57:17.072 --> 01:57:18.073
Odysseus.

1369
01:57:26.331 --> 01:57:27.374
Where am I?

1370
01:57:29.501 --> 01:57:31.295
Don't you recognize your own home?

1371
01:58:10.709 --> 01:58:12.753
Zeus welcomes you, strangers.

1372
01:58:13.545 --> 01:58:14.546
How many are you?

1373
01:58:15.130 --> 01:58:16.131
Half a dozen.

1374
01:58:16.840 --> 01:58:17.925
In need of a meal?

1375
01:58:18.008 --> 01:58:19.301
And supplies.

1376
01:58:20.010 --> 01:58:21.178
For the road.

1377
01:58:21.261 --> 01:58:22.513
Where you headed?

1378
01:58:22.596 --> 01:58:24.014
We need pigs.

1379
01:58:24.098 --> 01:58:25.808
We're going all the way to Pylos.

1380
01:58:25.891 --> 01:58:27.768
Are you pilgrims?

1381
01:58:27.851 --> 01:58:29.812
Do we look like pilgrims?

1382
01:58:29.895 --> 01:58:31.271
I don't see well.

1383
01:58:32.481 --> 01:58:35.984
As Zeus demands, I offer you a meal but these pigs aren't mine to sell.

1384
01:58:36.068 --> 01:58:37.444
Oh, don't worry.

1385
01:58:37.945 --> 01:58:38.779
We're not paying.

1386
01:58:42.449 --> 01:58:44.827
Give my greetings to your mother.

1387
01:58:47.871 --> 01:58:49.414
Tell her I'm sorry.

1388
01:58:49.498 --> 01:58:50.958
I'm sorry for Troy

1389
01:58:51.041 --> 01:58:53.252
and for everything that has happened in my name.

1390
01:58:59.842 --> 01:59:02.302
I'm sorry I had no news of your father.

1391
01:59:02.386 --> 01:59:03.428
No, you had news.

1392
01:59:03.512 --> 01:59:06.056
Not of where he is, but who he is.

1393
01:59:07.099 --> 01:59:09.643
And now, more than ever, I hope to one day meet him.

1394
01:59:14.773 --> 01:59:16.859
Where are your watchdogs, old man?

1395
01:59:21.321 --> 01:59:23.448
I'm burning their bodies.

1396
01:59:27.744 --> 01:59:30.581
Can Zeus offer you food and drink, stranger?

1397
01:59:31.498 --> 01:59:33.125
Were you attacked?

1398
01:59:33.208 --> 01:59:35.002
The dogs saved your farmhands.

1399
01:59:35.085 --> 01:59:37.588
No, we burned his brothers first.

1400
01:59:40.382 --> 01:59:42.301
What has happened to Ithaca?

1401
01:59:42.384 --> 01:59:43.385
Darkness.

1402
01:59:43.468 --> 01:59:46.346
Zeus' Law smashed to pieces.

1403
01:59:47.139 --> 01:59:50.309
A kingdom without a king since my master died.

1404
01:59:50.392 --> 01:59:51.393
Your master.

1405
01:59:52.436 --> 01:59:53.854
Odysseus.

1406
01:59:53.937 --> 01:59:55.981
Hero of the Trojan War.

1407
01:59:58.358 --> 01:59:59.985
But Odysseus is alive.

1408
02:00:00.068 --> 02:00:01.987
Beggars giving false hope!

1409
02:00:02.070 --> 02:00:04.156
No more! No more!

1410
02:00:04.239 --> 02:00:06.700
No more! No more. No more.

1411
02:00:06.783 --> 02:00:08.202
No more. No more.

1412
02:00:08.285 --> 02:00:11.371
Telemachus. Telemachus.

1413
02:00:11.455 --> 02:00:12.456
Telemachus.

1414
02:00:12.539 --> 02:00:15.792
He's in danger, and I can't help him.

1415
02:00:15.876 --> 02:00:19.504
There must be others in Ithaca that would help Odysseus' son.

1416
02:00:19.588 --> 02:00:22.049
The palace is controlled by suitors.

1417
02:00:22.633 --> 02:00:25.135
No one's still loyal to Odysseus.

1418
02:00:26.345 --> 02:00:27.679
What about the Queen?

1419
02:00:28.305 --> 02:00:30.682
Beautiful, wise Penelope.

1420
02:00:31.433 --> 02:00:33.769
Held hostage by the worst men.

1421
02:00:33.852 --> 02:00:36.104
Soon to be forced into marriage.

1422
02:00:36.188 --> 02:00:38.565
What could she do without even her son?

1423
02:00:38.649 --> 02:00:39.983
Here. Drink.

1424
02:00:44.112 --> 02:00:46.323
What's the danger to Telemachus?

1425
02:00:47.366 --> 02:00:49.534
He's on his way back from Sparta.

1426
02:00:50.744 --> 02:00:55.123
The suitors sent bandits to Pylos to ambush him at the temple.

1427
02:00:56.250 --> 02:00:57.709
Give me food for the road.

1428
02:00:58.210 --> 02:01:01.046
You'll... You'll never catch up with the bandits.

1429
02:01:01.129 --> 02:01:04.925
If I can get to Pylos before Telemachus arrives, I can warn him.

1430
02:01:05.008 --> 02:01:06.385
Why are you helping?

1431
02:01:07.344 --> 02:01:09.054
If I tell you, don't be angry.

1432
02:01:09.846 --> 02:01:11.556
I served with Odysseus. At Troy.

1433
02:01:11.640 --> 02:01:12.641
Really?

1434
02:01:14.101 --> 02:01:15.602
He was a harsh taskmaster.

1435
02:01:15.686 --> 02:01:16.770
Ah, but fair.

1436
02:01:18.230 --> 02:01:20.649
Fair and worthy of following.

1437
02:01:21.566 --> 02:01:22.567
You loved him?

1438
02:01:23.652 --> 02:01:24.987
Like a son.

1439
02:01:25.654 --> 02:01:28.031
Well, he's not dead.

1440
02:01:28.532 --> 02:01:30.742
Is... Is he coming home?

1441
02:01:30.826 --> 02:01:32.035
Soon.

1442
02:01:32.619 --> 02:01:34.788
Bringing vengeance.

1443
02:01:36.540 --> 02:01:38.000
Bringing it all.

1444
02:01:47.259 --> 02:01:50.053
I'll talk. We don't want people realizing who you are.

1445
02:01:50.137 --> 02:01:52.055
Well, maybe we shouldn't have brought a guard.

1446
02:01:52.806 --> 02:01:54.808
We'll be glad we did if we have trouble on the road.

1447
02:02:06.486 --> 02:02:07.571
Get back! Move!

1448
02:02:07.654 --> 02:02:09.656
I have a message. Wait!

1449
02:02:11.450 --> 02:02:12.492
Eumaeus sent me!

1450
02:02:14.703 --> 02:02:15.704
Find that beggar.

1451
02:02:32.137 --> 02:02:34.639
Your men must wait out here. The weapons too.

1452
02:02:51.490 --> 02:02:53.825
Zeus welcomes you, strangers.

1453
02:02:53.909 --> 02:02:55.660
Have you traveled far?

1454
02:02:55.744 --> 02:02:56.912
Not that far.

1455
02:02:58.121 --> 02:03:00.040
You're here to rest for the night.

1456
02:03:02.125 --> 02:03:04.378
We're here to make sacrifice to Athena.

1457
02:03:06.546 --> 02:03:09.508
And whatever hospitality you can offer might be considered.

1458
02:03:09.591 --> 02:03:11.802
Of course. Get that beggar out of here.

1459
02:03:11.885 --> 02:03:13.595
He has a message for me.

1460
02:03:14.596 --> 02:03:15.806
Eumaeus sent you?

1461
02:03:16.973 --> 02:03:18.058
Yes, sir.

1462
02:03:23.939 --> 02:03:25.399
Well, what's the message?

1463
02:03:26.608 --> 02:03:28.068
It's private.

1464
02:03:28.151 --> 02:03:29.736
These men are...

1465
02:03:30.695 --> 02:03:31.696
These men are priests.

1466
02:03:49.423 --> 02:03:51.800
This is your moment to leave, old man.

1467
02:03:51.883 --> 02:03:53.260
Go, stranger.

1468
02:03:56.138 --> 02:03:58.723
I already spared one old man this week.

1469
02:03:58.807 --> 02:03:59.975
But he was blind.

1470
02:04:00.058 --> 02:04:01.059
I won't spare...

1471
02:04:40.515 --> 02:04:42.434
Not bad for a beggar.

1472
02:04:42.517 --> 02:04:44.436
No one's born a beggar.

1473
02:04:44.519 --> 02:04:47.647
We'll take these priest robes, put 'em over your armor.

1474
02:04:49.816 --> 02:04:52.402
- Help me with his body. - We have to leave him.

1475
02:04:52.486 --> 02:04:55.030
- No. - We have to slip out.

1476
02:04:55.113 --> 02:04:57.824
- That's not how you honor the dead. - That is how you stay alive.

1477
02:04:57.908 --> 02:04:59.493
He was my teacher.

1478
02:05:00.577 --> 02:05:01.745
He might have been Athena in disguise.

1479
02:05:01.828 --> 02:05:03.330
His name was Mentor.

1480
02:05:04.080 --> 02:05:05.123
And when you are safe,

1481
02:05:05.207 --> 02:05:08.418
we'll travel to the unknown west and we will honor him.

1482
02:05:09.085 --> 02:05:11.046
These men were sent by the suitors.

1483
02:05:11.129 --> 02:05:13.465
There will be more of them. We need to get on the road.

1484
02:05:13.548 --> 02:05:15.550
- By night? - Safer than staying here.

1485
02:05:15.634 --> 02:05:18.386
Mentor's at the heart of Athena's oldest temple.

1486
02:05:19.012 --> 02:05:21.139
For now, we'll honor him in flame.

1487
02:05:30.899 --> 02:05:32.442
Fire!

1488
02:05:36.488 --> 02:05:38.406
Fire! Fire! Fire!

1489
02:05:38.490 --> 02:05:39.908
Fire! Fire!

1490
02:06:00.095 --> 02:06:02.013
So how does a beggar come to know Mentor?

1491
02:06:02.097 --> 02:06:04.683
I told you. I wasn't always a beggar.

1492
02:06:04.766 --> 02:06:06.601
You have wise eyes.

1493
02:06:07.269 --> 02:06:09.771
- Athena's eyes. - Don't look for gods in men.

1494
02:06:09.854 --> 02:06:11.606
You'll just be disappointed.

1495
02:06:11.690 --> 02:06:13.316
Well, then tell me who you are.

1496
02:06:14.901 --> 02:06:16.278
My name is Sinon.

1497
02:06:16.903 --> 02:06:20.115
I'm an old soldier who fought under your father at Troy.

1498
02:06:21.116 --> 02:06:22.659
You knew my father?

1499
02:06:22.742 --> 02:06:25.328
I've been taking orders from him for longer than I care to remember.

1500
02:06:26.830 --> 02:06:28.123
Wait, he's alive?

1501
02:06:29.124 --> 02:06:30.166
He's alive.

1502
02:06:31.501 --> 02:06:32.794
Well, where is he?

1503
02:06:33.295 --> 02:06:34.504
He's close by.

1504
02:06:35.255 --> 02:06:37.507
He sent me ahead to assess the situation.

1505
02:06:39.009 --> 02:06:40.719
Well, Mother's not gonna believe it.

1506
02:06:40.802 --> 02:06:42.178
No, you can't tell her.

1507
02:06:43.346 --> 02:06:44.639
What, nothing?

1508
02:06:45.599 --> 02:06:46.808
Tell her...

1509
02:06:47.809 --> 02:06:50.228
Tell her you decided it's time she remarried.

1510
02:06:52.063 --> 02:06:53.315
He doesn't need to test my mother.

1511
02:06:53.398 --> 02:06:54.524
How could he doubt her?

1512
02:06:54.608 --> 02:06:56.067
He doesn't.

1513
02:06:56.151 --> 02:06:58.528
But she has every reason to have forgotten him.

1514
02:07:00.363 --> 02:07:01.948
Are there any loyal servants?

1515
02:07:02.991 --> 02:07:04.075
Some.

1516
02:07:04.951 --> 02:07:06.411
When Odysseus shows himself, all.

1517
02:07:08.455 --> 02:07:10.624
We need to gather the suitors at the palace.

1518
02:07:11.416 --> 02:07:13.251
That's never a problem.

1519
02:07:13.335 --> 02:07:15.295
You have gifts from Menelaus?

1520
02:07:15.378 --> 02:07:17.672
- Yeah, on my ship. - Good.

1521
02:07:18.465 --> 02:07:19.591
Come back as a prince.

1522
02:07:20.091 --> 02:07:21.301
Call a banquet.

1523
02:07:21.801 --> 02:07:22.927
I'll be there.

1524
02:07:23.928 --> 02:07:25.388
What will you do at the banquet?

1525
02:07:26.014 --> 02:07:27.474
Beg.

1526
02:07:27.557 --> 02:07:29.768
The clearest view of a man is from below.

1527
02:07:31.645 --> 02:07:33.063
How many suitors are there?

1528
02:07:33.605 --> 02:07:34.856
Dozens.

1529
02:07:35.523 --> 02:07:36.983
But if all of his men are like you, then...

1530
02:07:37.067 --> 02:07:38.526
He doesn't have any men.

1531
02:07:39.402 --> 02:07:40.654
What, it's just you?

1532
02:07:41.863 --> 02:07:42.906
And you.

1533
02:07:48.912 --> 02:07:50.121
Telemachus is back.

1534
02:07:50.205 --> 02:07:51.915
- What? - Telemachus is back.

1535
02:07:52.666 --> 02:07:55.126
- Who's saying this? - Everyone's saying it.

1536
02:07:55.210 --> 02:07:56.336
Where's Telemachus?

1537
02:07:57.253 --> 02:07:59.839
My Queen! Surely your place is up there?

1538
02:08:08.014 --> 02:08:09.224
It's true?

1539
02:08:12.060 --> 02:08:13.061
You're home.

1540
02:08:14.020 --> 02:08:15.105
I'm back.

1541
02:08:15.689 --> 02:08:17.148
I thought tonight we could celebrate.

1542
02:08:18.316 --> 02:08:21.027
If we have anything left to celebrate with.

1543
02:08:22.570 --> 02:08:24.030
Don't worry.

1544
02:08:24.114 --> 02:08:26.700
My brave suitors will shower us with gifts to honor your return.

1545
02:08:27.283 --> 02:08:28.952
So glad to see your safe return.

1546
02:08:39.838 --> 02:08:41.381
Glad to see you on your feet.

1547
02:08:42.090 --> 02:08:43.967
It's about time we took you back to the palace.

1548
02:08:47.637 --> 02:08:49.347
What did you hear about your father?

1549
02:08:51.015 --> 02:08:52.016
Nothing.

1550
02:08:52.851 --> 02:08:53.935
Stories from the war,

1551
02:08:54.018 --> 02:08:56.271
but no one knew what happened to him after he left Troy.

1552
02:08:57.021 --> 02:08:58.148
I'm sorry, Mom.

1553
02:08:59.065 --> 02:09:02.861
Everyone in Sparta is talking about a coming catastrophe.

1554
02:09:03.611 --> 02:09:07.449
An age of darkness brought on by people from the sea violating Zeus' Law.

1555
02:09:07.532 --> 02:09:08.908
So the stories are true.

1556
02:09:08.992 --> 02:09:10.952
Our civilization is collapsing.

1557
02:09:11.453 --> 02:09:13.079
Menelaus is concerned that we can't raise an army

1558
02:09:13.163 --> 02:09:14.497
with an empty throne.

1559
02:09:14.581 --> 02:09:16.040
So we came up with a plan.

1560
02:09:17.667 --> 02:09:18.668
What plan?

1561
02:09:20.837 --> 02:09:22.922
The one way to get the suitors to leave.

1562
02:09:25.216 --> 02:09:26.718
It's time for you to choose.

1563
02:09:30.597 --> 02:09:32.182
You want me to remarry?

1564
02:09:32.265 --> 02:09:35.018
If you remarry, the other suitors will leave.

1565
02:09:35.101 --> 02:09:37.020
And then I'm only dealing with one of them.

1566
02:09:37.103 --> 02:09:38.104
I'm almost of age.

1567
02:09:38.188 --> 02:09:41.232
I'll tell the elders that I learned of Odysseus' death in Sparta.

1568
02:09:42.150 --> 02:09:45.153
Menelaus will back me up, and they'll let me assume the throne.

1569
02:09:45.236 --> 02:09:47.363
So you deny your father, and I leave my home

1570
02:09:47.447 --> 02:09:48.782
and go live with one of these snakes.

1571
02:09:48.865 --> 02:09:49.866
That's your plan.

1572
02:09:49.949 --> 02:09:51.493
Well, do you have a better one?

1573
02:09:54.162 --> 02:09:57.540
Lock the door, set a fire, and burn the bastards.

1574
02:09:58.625 --> 02:10:02.337
Do you think if I remarry life goes back to the way it was?

1575
02:10:03.296 --> 02:10:05.256
That world is gone.

1576
02:10:05.340 --> 02:10:06.382
And, in this one,

1577
02:10:06.466 --> 02:10:08.426
they're not giving up power to you.

1578
02:10:08.510 --> 02:10:09.511
Or Odysseus.

1579
02:10:09.594 --> 02:10:11.805
If he returned, he'd have to kill them.

1580
02:10:12.305 --> 02:10:13.473
Like you will.

1581
02:10:14.182 --> 02:10:17.101
If I kill them, their families will demand revenge.

1582
02:10:17.185 --> 02:10:18.561
I'd be exiled.

1583
02:10:19.479 --> 02:10:21.105
And, without me, the throne'll be lost.

1584
02:10:21.189 --> 02:10:22.941
'Cause only a man can sit on it.

1585
02:10:24.734 --> 02:10:25.777
Empty throne?

1586
02:10:27.695 --> 02:10:31.199
I've been sitting on that empty throne for 20 years!

1587
02:10:31.282 --> 02:10:33.910
My knowledge, my years of experience

1588
02:10:33.993 --> 02:10:36.746
that's all nothing compared to the bristles on your chin.

1589
02:10:39.582 --> 02:10:40.875
Do you want peace?

1590
02:10:42.001 --> 02:10:43.711
- Or do you want revenge? - I want Odysseus!

1591
02:10:47.298 --> 02:10:48.383
Sing...

1592
02:10:49.050 --> 02:10:50.426
Muse, of a face...

1593
02:10:53.847 --> 02:10:55.056
...a face...

1594
02:10:59.394 --> 02:11:00.478
...a fleet...

1595
02:11:04.816 --> 02:11:05.859
...a war...

1596
02:11:10.196 --> 02:11:11.364
...at Troy...

1597
02:11:15.410 --> 02:11:17.370
Sing, Muse, of a man...

1598
02:11:21.666 --> 02:11:22.709
...a man...

1599
02:11:26.963 --> 02:11:28.047
...a thought...

1600
02:11:32.051 --> 02:11:33.261
...a trick...

1601
02:11:36.973 --> 02:11:38.099
Eumaeus, go ahead.

1602
02:11:45.023 --> 02:11:46.274
Argus.

1603
02:12:09.172 --> 02:12:11.215
You should bury him up on the cliff.

1604
02:12:13.176 --> 02:12:14.385
Where you met?

1605
02:12:23.102 --> 02:12:24.437
You're Odysseus.

1606
02:12:27.523 --> 02:12:29.067
You're my father.

1607
02:12:31.611 --> 02:12:32.695
I'm a beggar.

1608
02:12:36.199 --> 02:12:37.325
And you

1609
02:12:39.369 --> 02:12:40.745
are the worthy,

1610
02:12:42.080 --> 02:12:45.041
beautiful son of Odysseus.

1611
02:12:46.709 --> 02:12:49.963
Of whom, when you do finally meet,

1612
02:12:52.048 --> 02:12:53.591
he will be so proud.

1613
02:12:57.553 --> 02:13:00.181
Welcome home, stranger.

1614
02:13:03.643 --> 02:13:05.228
Because the darkness of victory

1615
02:13:06.521 --> 02:13:08.606
shrouds the writers of legends...

1616
02:13:10.733 --> 02:13:13.277
...and make bards and blind beggars of us all.

1617
02:13:20.785 --> 02:13:22.704
Accept the hospitality of Zeus.

1618
02:13:23.371 --> 02:13:25.206
Get the stranger some food.

1619
02:13:25.289 --> 02:13:26.290
No, sir.

1620
02:13:27.125 --> 02:13:29.168
I'll just make the rounds with a bowl.

1621
02:13:34.590 --> 02:13:37.218
Had to invite another one of these filthy plate-lickers to dinner?

1622
02:13:37.301 --> 02:13:39.429
What's one more beggar at this feast?

1623
02:13:57.321 --> 02:13:59.824
We need to kill him before he regains his position with the elders.

1624
02:13:59.907 --> 02:14:01.117
How?

1625
02:14:16.466 --> 02:14:17.467
Bring the wine! Bring it!

1626
02:14:17.550 --> 02:14:19.010
Provoke him.

1627
02:14:19.594 --> 02:14:21.429
Get him to grab one of the weapons.

1628
02:14:25.683 --> 02:14:28.478
He's not the same young pup we used to bait.

1629
02:14:28.561 --> 02:14:30.897
Then we come back tonight, and we cut his throat.

1630
02:14:32.523 --> 02:14:34.150
- Fuck off, old man. - Please.

1631
02:14:34.984 --> 02:14:36.277
It's been days since I've eaten.

1632
02:14:36.360 --> 02:14:37.361
I told you to fuck off!

1633
02:14:37.445 --> 02:14:41.074
You can't be generous with food that's not even yours?

1634
02:14:42.742 --> 02:14:44.118
There's no room in your bowl.

1635
02:14:44.202 --> 02:14:45.453
Yes, there is.

1636
02:14:50.666 --> 02:14:52.376
So there is.

1637
02:14:53.961 --> 02:14:55.880
There you go.

1638
02:14:59.300 --> 02:15:00.885
Suppose you want something from me now.

1639
02:15:00.968 --> 02:15:02.011
No.

1640
02:15:02.637 --> 02:15:04.388
I have something for you.

1641
02:15:05.348 --> 02:15:07.725
You have something for me?

1642
02:15:07.809 --> 02:15:09.352
He has something for me!

1643
02:15:11.229 --> 02:15:14.690
What could a filthy old plate-licker like you

1644
02:15:14.774 --> 02:15:17.610
possibly have that I could want?

1645
02:15:17.693 --> 02:15:20.363
Actually, it's yours.

1646
02:15:25.743 --> 02:15:27.495
I was asked to return it.

1647
02:15:29.622 --> 02:15:30.665
It's not mine.

1648
02:15:32.542 --> 02:15:33.584
No?

1649
02:15:36.921 --> 02:15:38.131
Where did you get this?

1650
02:15:40.508 --> 02:15:42.343
Don't turn your back on me!

1651
02:15:47.849 --> 02:15:50.893
Antinous, you can't treat a beggar like that.

1652
02:15:50.977 --> 02:15:52.019
It's Zeus' Law.

1653
02:15:52.603 --> 02:15:53.896
Zeus' Law.

1654
02:15:58.401 --> 02:15:59.735
You think this is a god?

1655
02:16:03.322 --> 02:16:05.199
Think this is a god in disguise?

1656
02:16:06.033 --> 02:16:07.034
Are you?

1657
02:16:08.828 --> 02:16:10.079
Are you a god,

1658
02:16:11.205 --> 02:16:12.248
plate-licker?

1659
02:16:13.416 --> 02:16:14.417
No.

1660
02:16:15.168 --> 02:16:17.253
I'm a veteran of the Trojan War.

1661
02:16:17.336 --> 02:16:19.964
Veteran of the Trojan War.

1662
02:16:22.550 --> 02:16:24.051
And what's your name, veteran?

1663
02:16:28.306 --> 02:16:29.307
Sinon.

1664
02:16:32.977 --> 02:16:34.645
Does that name mean something to you?

1665
02:16:35.605 --> 02:16:36.647
No.

1666
02:16:37.690 --> 02:16:41.819
I use it to honor the bravest young man I ever met.

1667
02:16:42.445 --> 02:16:43.529
Sinon.

1668
02:16:45.156 --> 02:16:46.824
Wasn't that your shepherd's boy?

1669
02:16:49.368 --> 02:16:50.453
I don't remember that.

1670
02:16:50.536 --> 02:16:53.164
Yeah, yeah, he wanted you to take his place.

1671
02:16:53.664 --> 02:16:54.999
Where did you find this?

1672
02:16:55.499 --> 02:16:56.834
Where you'll soon be.

1673
02:16:57.835 --> 02:16:58.920
And where is that?

1674
02:16:59.545 --> 02:17:00.838
Hades.

1675
02:17:04.342 --> 02:17:05.676
Enough!

1676
02:17:07.136 --> 02:17:08.804
The feast is done!

1677
02:17:13.142 --> 02:17:14.060
Let's go.

1678
02:17:15.311 --> 02:17:16.729
It's not mine!

1679
02:17:25.571 --> 02:17:27.531
The Queen would like to speak with you.

1680
02:17:27.615 --> 02:17:30.076
Stay till everyone's left and the fires are lit.

1681
02:18:21.002 --> 02:18:22.295
Hello, traveler.

1682
02:18:23.421 --> 02:18:24.714
My Queen.

1683
02:18:24.797 --> 02:18:28.676
Melantho, have Eurycleia wash the stranger's feet.

1684
02:18:28.759 --> 02:18:30.094
That's not necessary.

1685
02:18:30.177 --> 02:18:33.764
My husband insisted we honor guests as family.

1686
02:18:34.390 --> 02:18:36.767
You know of Odysseus, my husband?

1687
02:18:36.851 --> 02:18:37.893
Of course.

1688
02:18:38.394 --> 02:18:39.645
Through the songs.

1689
02:18:40.229 --> 02:18:41.814
I was at Troy, ma'am.

1690
02:18:42.481 --> 02:18:44.483
Then the songs probably seem silly.

1691
02:18:45.985 --> 02:18:47.403
They make me cry.

1692
02:18:48.446 --> 02:18:49.613
Why?

1693
02:18:49.697 --> 02:18:51.532
For what was lost.

1694
02:18:52.408 --> 02:18:53.576
The lives?

1695
02:18:54.243 --> 02:18:55.536
And the years.

1696
02:18:56.704 --> 02:18:57.705
Everything.

1697
02:19:05.338 --> 02:19:07.506
Did you know Odysseus at Troy?

1698
02:19:07.590 --> 02:19:08.758
I did.

1699
02:19:10.009 --> 02:19:13.929
Was there some detail of how he dressed, something he wore?

1700
02:19:14.638 --> 02:19:16.599
He wore a strange pin.

1701
02:19:16.682 --> 02:19:18.225
Strange?

1702
02:19:18.768 --> 02:19:19.769
Beautiful.

1703
02:19:20.644 --> 02:19:21.854
But out of place.

1704
02:19:22.355 --> 02:19:23.356
Describe it.

1705
02:19:37.870 --> 02:19:39.455
I'll get more water.

1706
02:19:43.751 --> 02:19:46.462
It was a beautiful, gold pin.

1707
02:19:47.755 --> 02:19:50.591
A figure of Athena encircled by vines.

1708
02:19:59.308 --> 02:20:00.893
Did he wear it often?

1709
02:20:02.269 --> 02:20:03.521
He never took it off.

1710
02:20:23.916 --> 02:20:26.710
How is it that you made it home when Odysseus could not?

1711
02:20:27.878 --> 02:20:30.589
I'm not home. Not yet.

1712
02:20:31.465 --> 02:20:32.716
Why not?

1713
02:20:32.800 --> 02:20:35.428
It's not always an easy thing, a homecoming.

1714
02:20:36.262 --> 02:20:38.848
That's as true for Odysseus as for me.

1715
02:20:40.141 --> 02:20:41.767
The Odysseus I knew,

1716
02:20:42.852 --> 02:20:47.606
would have found or fought his way back to me no matter what.

1717
02:20:48.524 --> 02:20:51.902
What if the Odysseus you knew lost his way?

1718
02:20:56.449 --> 02:20:59.827
What if, one night,

1719
02:21:00.953 --> 02:21:02.371
in a strange city,

1720
02:21:04.206 --> 02:21:05.458
he saw things

1721
02:21:06.959 --> 02:21:09.420
that made him think the home he knew

1722
02:21:10.754 --> 02:21:12.798
couldn't possibly be there anymore?

1723
02:21:16.844 --> 02:21:17.720
What if,

1724
02:21:18.345 --> 02:21:20.264
when he left the belly of the horse

1725
02:21:24.602 --> 02:21:26.437
and opened the gates of Troy,

1726
02:21:31.901 --> 02:21:34.653
he saw ten years of rage

1727
02:21:34.737 --> 02:21:37.573
pour into that city in one night?

1728
02:22:25.037 --> 02:22:27.456
Everyone, now!

1729
02:23:53.334 --> 02:23:56.420
We left them a gift.

1730
02:23:56.503 --> 02:23:59.173
An offering of peace.

1731
02:23:59.256 --> 02:24:02.509
That they took into their home.

1732
02:24:12.728 --> 02:24:15.564
We violated all that's ever sacred

1733
02:24:16.899 --> 02:24:18.484
between people.

1734
02:24:20.444 --> 02:24:21.487
And turned a fight...

1735
02:24:22.446 --> 02:24:23.656
Destroy their idols!

1736
02:24:25.449 --> 02:24:26.700
...into a hunt.

1737
02:24:31.038 --> 02:24:32.039
Please don't.

1738
02:24:42.675 --> 02:24:44.593
To burn the walls of Troy

1739
02:24:45.219 --> 02:24:47.680
was to burn the world entire.

1740
02:24:49.431 --> 02:24:51.100
Including his home.

1741
02:24:57.856 --> 02:24:59.024
What if he knew

1742
02:25:00.859 --> 02:25:02.194
that very night...

1743
02:25:05.364 --> 02:25:09.660
...as he walked through fires of anarchy and pain...

1744
02:25:58.917 --> 02:26:02.337
...and in the days of sweaty celebrations to follow...

1745
02:26:08.677 --> 02:26:11.889
...what if he knew exactly what he'd done?

1746
02:26:30.532 --> 02:26:32.326
What all of you had done.

1747
02:26:32.409 --> 02:26:34.077
One man's idea.

1748
02:26:35.579 --> 02:26:37.080
One man's trick.

1749
02:26:38.707 --> 02:26:41.418
To break Zeus' Law forever.

1750
02:26:42.795 --> 02:26:45.964
We lived in a world of palaces and trade.

1751
02:26:47.633 --> 02:26:48.801
And language.

1752
02:26:48.884 --> 02:26:50.677
Blind to its beauty.

1753
02:26:51.637 --> 02:26:53.263
Until we broke it.

1754
02:26:55.682 --> 02:26:57.893
You are the people from the sea?

1755
02:27:00.479 --> 02:27:01.605
Yes, my Queen.

1756
02:27:03.357 --> 02:27:06.693
The breaking of Zeus' Law is spreading like plague.

1757
02:27:06.777 --> 02:27:08.445
Our age of bronze is collapsing.

1758
02:27:08.529 --> 02:27:12.157
And maybe he couldn't bear to see the ruins of what he'd done.

1759
02:27:12.991 --> 02:27:13.992
Anywhere.

1760
02:27:14.535 --> 02:27:16.578
Least of all, his home.

1761
02:27:26.171 --> 02:27:27.923
But Odysseus is alive.

1762
02:27:29.716 --> 02:27:31.885
And he'll be here when you need him most.

1763
02:27:34.012 --> 02:27:35.180
Well,

1764
02:27:36.515 --> 02:27:39.351
I've been living in his ruins long enough.

1765
02:27:44.606 --> 02:27:46.108
Bring the suitors at dawn.

1766
02:27:46.191 --> 02:27:48.235
Remove the arms from the hall.

1767
02:27:48.318 --> 02:27:52.573
In a few hours, one suitor will be happy and the rest angry, vengeful men.

1768
02:27:52.656 --> 02:27:54.116
Bronze draws a man on,

1769
02:27:54.199 --> 02:27:57.077
so we'll have no weapons at the trial and the doors will be locked.

1770
02:27:57.953 --> 02:28:00.205
I'll marry whoever completes my challenge.

1771
02:28:06.920 --> 02:28:08.547
How will we take on the suitors?

1772
02:28:09.506 --> 02:28:10.757
Not "we."

1773
02:28:11.300 --> 02:28:13.260
Their blood belongs on my hands.

1774
02:28:13.844 --> 02:28:15.262
Or you face exile.

1775
02:28:16.305 --> 02:28:17.431
Won't you?

1776
02:28:18.348 --> 02:28:20.684
Just make sure they can't get those weapons.

1777
02:28:20.767 --> 02:28:21.977
I'll see to it.

1778
02:28:22.728 --> 02:28:24.730
Even unarmed, there are so many of them.

1779
02:28:25.522 --> 02:28:27.065
And you won't have a weapon.

1780
02:28:28.525 --> 02:28:29.985
Oh, I'll have a weapon.

1781
02:28:31.695 --> 02:28:33.071
Your mother's seen to that.

1782
02:28:35.657 --> 02:28:37.910
Gather the servants you trust.

1783
02:28:37.993 --> 02:28:39.828
Lock the arms in the upper storage room.

1784
02:28:39.912 --> 02:28:41.038
Yes, I'll see to it.

1785
02:28:59.222 --> 02:29:01.558
Hurry, hurry. Get the weapons upstairs.

1786
02:29:09.191 --> 02:29:10.400
Hurry, hurry.

1787
02:29:33.090 --> 02:29:35.050
Zeus seems interested in the outcome.

1788
02:29:35.133 --> 02:29:36.510
But what's the trial?

1789
02:30:00.993 --> 02:30:02.411
For years,

1790
02:30:03.537 --> 02:30:05.163
while my husband's been gone,

1791
02:30:05.914 --> 02:30:08.750
you've commandeered this palace for your feasting.

1792
02:30:09.418 --> 02:30:12.629
Your excuse was you wanted to win my hand in marriage.

1793
02:30:16.258 --> 02:30:20.178
As I listened to you party and fight,

1794
02:30:22.514 --> 02:30:26.852
corrupt my servants and disrespect my home,

1795
02:30:26.935 --> 02:30:29.229
I've wanted nothing more than for Odysseus to return

1796
02:30:29.312 --> 02:30:31.023
and see all this for himself.

1797
02:30:37.988 --> 02:30:40.949
But, last night, I realized

1798
02:30:41.033 --> 02:30:42.325
he's not here now.

1799
02:30:46.079 --> 02:30:47.289
He never will be.

1800
02:30:49.249 --> 02:30:50.375
So,

1801
02:30:51.168 --> 02:30:54.379
I stand ready to marry anyone here

1802
02:30:54.463 --> 02:30:59.760
who can string this hunting bow and shoot an arrow through those 12 axes.

1803
02:31:01.553 --> 02:31:03.055
Step up.

1804
02:31:04.890 --> 02:31:06.183
And show me who you are.

1805
02:31:07.809 --> 02:31:08.810
Eumaeus.

1806
02:31:20.572 --> 02:31:22.074
Who's first?

1807
02:31:22.157 --> 02:31:23.241
Give me the bow.

1808
02:31:43.929 --> 02:31:44.971
It can't be done.

1809
02:31:45.055 --> 02:31:46.431
By you.

1810
02:31:46.515 --> 02:31:48.683
As a boy, I saw Odysseus do it with ease.

1811
02:31:48.767 --> 02:31:49.810
Then you do it.

1812
02:31:50.769 --> 02:31:51.770
I'll wait my turn.

1813
02:31:53.063 --> 02:31:55.315
Why don't we warm the bow and oil it?

1814
02:31:56.775 --> 02:31:58.735
This bow hasn't been strung in years.

1815
02:32:32.227 --> 02:32:33.270
Your turn.

1816
02:32:33.353 --> 02:32:34.688
There's no need.

1817
02:32:37.983 --> 02:32:39.484
My Queen has made her point.

1818
02:32:39.568 --> 02:32:41.528
We can't replace Odysseus.

1819
02:32:41.611 --> 02:32:43.238
Some of us have always known that.

1820
02:32:43.321 --> 02:32:46.241
Some of us would have followed that great man to Troy.

1821
02:32:46.324 --> 02:32:47.367
If he'd let us.

1822
02:32:50.203 --> 02:32:51.705
But he never came back.

1823
02:32:53.623 --> 02:32:55.208
So choose the best of us here.

1824
02:32:56.877 --> 02:32:59.212
All of us who've failed your test.

1825
02:32:59.796 --> 02:33:00.964
I haven't.

1826
02:33:05.844 --> 02:33:07.512
Somebody get these beggars out of here!

1827
02:33:08.680 --> 02:33:11.600
The doors are locked until the trial's over.

1828
02:33:11.683 --> 02:33:14.227
- The trial is over. - Yeah.

1829
02:33:14.311 --> 02:33:15.353
I wanna try.

1830
02:33:15.437 --> 02:33:17.564
Give it a rest, old man.

1831
02:33:18.732 --> 02:33:20.567
No, he has a right to try.

1832
02:33:21.776 --> 02:33:23.904
If you won't, he may as well.

1833
02:33:24.404 --> 02:33:26.364
You're making a mockery of your mother's wishes.

1834
02:33:26.448 --> 02:33:28.867
My mother's wishes are for you all to burn.

1835
02:33:40.462 --> 02:33:42.714
He's an old archer.

1836
02:33:45.967 --> 02:33:47.177
Come on.

1837
02:33:47.802 --> 02:33:49.888
Look at the expert at work.

1838
02:33:49.971 --> 02:33:52.015
Plate-licker!

1839
02:33:52.807 --> 02:33:53.934
Come on!

1840
02:33:54.017 --> 02:33:55.685
Old man, forget it!

1841
02:33:56.728 --> 02:33:57.854
Hey, old man!

1842
02:33:58.563 --> 02:33:59.981
Come on!

1843
02:34:07.113 --> 02:34:08.281
Okay, friend.

1844
02:34:08.782 --> 02:34:09.908
Friend, that's enough.

1845
02:34:09.991 --> 02:34:11.243
Let him try.

1846
02:34:11.326 --> 02:34:12.994
- Let him try. - Come on.

1847
02:34:54.869 --> 02:34:56.204
You're Odyss...

1848
02:35:02.085 --> 02:35:04.796
Surely your place is down there.

1849
02:35:18.810 --> 02:35:19.811
Is it him?

1850
02:35:20.854 --> 02:35:21.688
It's him.

1851
02:35:31.656 --> 02:35:32.782
Go secure the weapons.

1852
02:35:47.422 --> 02:35:48.840
Where are the weapons?

1853
02:35:48.923 --> 02:35:49.924
The upper store room.

1854
02:35:50.008 --> 02:35:51.009
Get them!

1855
02:35:55.221 --> 02:35:58.475
What are you waiting for? He's gonna kill us all!

1856
02:36:01.936 --> 02:36:03.188
Look out!

1857
02:36:08.401 --> 02:36:10.862
I told you. Too loud, cowherd.

1858
02:36:10.945 --> 02:36:12.906
No! No! No!

1859
02:36:13.531 --> 02:36:16.242
Eumaeus! Let me out!

1860
02:36:21.581 --> 02:36:22.582
Down!

1861
02:36:33.426 --> 02:36:34.636
No!

1862
02:37:29.816 --> 02:37:31.151
Get the swords!

1863
02:38:31.002 --> 02:38:33.087
Get the shields! Get the shields!

1864
02:39:12.085 --> 02:39:13.378
Spears!

1865
02:39:43.283 --> 02:39:44.659
Use the spears!

1866
02:40:38.713 --> 02:40:40.089
Thought you could kill us all?

1867
02:40:40.590 --> 02:40:42.216
Long as I kill you.

1868
02:40:43.801 --> 02:40:44.844
Finish this.

1869
02:40:48.014 --> 02:40:49.432
Dad!

1870
02:41:19.295 --> 02:41:20.296
When you get to Hades,

1871
02:41:22.799 --> 02:41:26.427
tell my men I will head west and honor them.

1872
02:41:32.475 --> 02:41:33.476
And tell Sinon

1873
02:41:36.270 --> 02:41:38.690
I gave you back your shame.

1874
02:41:58.042 --> 02:41:59.419
What happened?

1875
02:42:00.586 --> 02:42:01.796
Did he beat them?

1876
02:42:03.047 --> 02:42:04.382
Is he dead?

1877
02:42:06.175 --> 02:42:08.845
We broke the fragile bonds between men.

1878
02:42:17.520 --> 02:42:20.606
And, for centuries of darkness to follow,

1879
02:42:20.690 --> 02:42:24.569
the stories of Troy will only be sung...

1880
02:43:44.524 --> 02:43:45.525
Odysseus, my love.

1881
02:43:46.859 --> 02:43:47.860
Please, my love.

1882
02:43:49.737 --> 02:43:51.489
Odysseus.

1883
02:43:51.572 --> 02:43:53.991
My love. You came back.

1884
02:43:55.660 --> 02:43:56.869
At last.

1885
02:43:57.578 --> 02:43:58.663
At the end.

1886
02:44:00.206 --> 02:44:04.252
To see my beloved Ithaca. One last time.

1887
02:44:05.211 --> 02:44:06.712
No. No.

1888
02:44:06.796 --> 02:44:07.797
No, no, you can't. You can't.

1889
02:44:07.880 --> 02:44:09.590
You can't. Not now. Not now.

1890
02:44:09.674 --> 02:44:11.092
Please. I've waited so long.

1891
02:44:13.553 --> 02:44:14.887
You can't die.

1892
02:44:16.180 --> 02:44:17.265
Not death.

1893
02:44:19.308 --> 02:44:20.601
Exile.

1894
02:44:28.860 --> 02:44:30.611
Telemachus will be King.

1895
02:44:34.282 --> 02:44:36.158
We'll head into the unknown west.

1896
02:44:37.159 --> 02:44:38.703
To honor my men.

1897
02:44:40.288 --> 02:44:41.414
Together.

1898
02:44:42.039 --> 02:44:43.875
Take your fastest ship...

1899
02:44:44.625 --> 02:44:46.669
...and brightest crew.

1900
02:44:46.752 --> 02:44:48.713
And head for the horizon.

1901
02:44:49.714 --> 02:44:51.757
Chasing the escaping sun.

1902
02:44:51.841 --> 02:44:54.218
A face...

1903
02:44:54.302 --> 02:44:56.220
Why will the stories only be sung?

1904
02:44:59.265 --> 02:45:02.685
Because songs will be all they have to remember those of us who could write.

1905
02:45:03.853 --> 02:45:06.230
...a war...

1906
02:45:07.607 --> 02:45:09.859
Civilization will rise again.

1907
02:45:09.942 --> 02:45:12.236
...a man...

1908
02:45:15.865 --> 02:45:18.701
A new dawn will break over the darkened world.

1909
02:45:22.121 --> 02:45:24.165
And our mistakes will once again be forgotten.

1910
02:45:24.248 --> 02:45:26.083
...a trick...
